theses at each possible position\n    for i in range(1, len(positions) + 1):\n        for combo in itertools.combinations(positions, i):\n            # Insert parentheses at the specified positions\n            expr_with_parentheses = expr[:combo[0]] + \'(\' + expr[combo[0]:]\n            for j in range(1, i):\n                expr_with_parentheses = expr_with_parentheses[:combo[j]] + \'(\' + expr_with_parentheses[combo[j]:]\n            expr_with_parentheses = expr_with_parentheses[:combo[i-1]] + \')\' + expr_with_parentheses[combo[i-1]:]\n            all_insertions.add(expr_with_parentheses)\n    \n    return all_insertions\n\n# Original expression as a string\noriginal_expr = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Generate all possible expressions with parentheses\nall_expressions = generate_all_parentheses_insertions(original_expr)\n\n# Evaluate each expression and collect unique values\nunique_values = set()\nfor expr in all_expressions:\n    value = evaluate_expression(expr)\n    if value is not None:\n        unique_values.add(value)\n\n# The number of unique values obtained\nnum_unique_values = len(unique_values)\nprint(num_unique_values)\n```\n```output\nIndexError: tuple index out of range\n```\nIt appears there\'s still an issue with the code. Let\'s simplify the approach and manually generate all possible ways of inserting parentheses. We can do this by considering all possible positions to insert parentheses and then evaluating each resulting expression.\n\nHere\'s a more straightforward approach:\n1. Generate all possible ways of inserting parentheses.\n2. Evaluate each resulting expression.\n3. Collect the unique values obtained from these evaluations.\n\nLet\'s try this approach:\n```python\nimport itertools\n\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    try:\n        return eval(expr)\n    except (SyntaxError, ValueError):\n        return None\n\n# Original expression as a string\noriginal_expr = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Generate all possible positions to insert parenthe