\n2. Evaluate each expression to get the result.\n3. Use a set to store the unique results.\n\nLet\'s write the Python code to achieve this:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression components\nexpression = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with different parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    # Split the expression into a list of tokens\n    tokens = expr.split()\n    # Generate all possible ways to insert parentheses\n    for i in range(1, len(tokens), 2):\n        # Insert parentheses in different places\n        for j in range(i+1, len(tokens)):\n            left_expr = "({} * {})".format(tokens[0], tokens[1])\n            right_expr = "({} * {})".format(tokens[2], tokens[3])\n            full_expr = "({} * {} + {})".format(left_expr, right_expr, tokens[4])\n            yield eval(full_expr)\n\n# Set to store unique results\nunique_results = set()\n\n# Evaluate all possible expressions\nfor placement in itertools.permutations(tokens, 2):\n    full_expr = "({} * {}) * ({} * {} + {})".format(placement[0], placement[1], placement[2], placement[3], tokens[4])\n    result = eval(full_expr)\n    unique_results.add(result)\n\n# The number of unique results\nnum_unique_results = len(unique_results)\nprint(num_unique_results)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name \'tokens\' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the variable `tokens` not being defined. Let\'s fix this by properly defining the tokens and then evaluating the expressions with different placements of parentheses.\n\nHere\'s the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression components\nexpression = "2 * 3 * 4 * 5 + 1"\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with different parentheses placements\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    # Split the expression into a list of tokens\n    tokens = expr.split()\n    # Generate all possible ways to insert parentheses\n    for i in range(1, len(tokens), 2):\n        # 