ut regard to the order of selection.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[ \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 4 upper class soldiers from 5:\n\\[ \\binom{5}{4} = \\frac{5!}{4!(5-4)!} = \\frac{5!}{4! \\cdot 1!} = 5 \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 8 lower class soldiers from 10:\n\\[ \\binom{10}{8} = \\frac{10!}{8!(10-8)!} = \\frac{10!}{8! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{10 \\cdot 9}{2 \\cdot 1} = 45 \\]\n\nThe total number of different battalions is the product of these two combinations:\n\\[ \\binom{5}{4} \\cdot \\binom{10}{8} = 5 \\cdot 45 = 225 \\]\n\nSo, the number of different battalions that can be sent is \\( \\boxed{225} \\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate combinations\nupper_class_combinations = math.comb(5, 4)\nlower_class_combinations = math.comb(10, 8)\n\n# Total number of different battalions\ntotal_battalions = upper_class_combinations * lower_class_combinations\nprint(total_battalions)\n```", "To determine the number of different battalions that can be sent, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 4 upper class soldiers from 5 and 8 lower class soldiers from 10. This is a combinatorial problem where we need to use the binomial coefficient, which is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\n\nHere, we need to calculate:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{4} \\times \\binom{10}{8}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(\\binom{5}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{4} = \\frac{5!}{4!(5-4)!} = \\frac{5!}{4! \\cdot 1!} = 5\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\binom{10}{8}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{8} = \\frac{10!}{8!(10-8)!} = \\frac{10!}{8! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{10 \\cdot 9}{2 \\cdot 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nFinally, we multiply these two results together:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{4} \\times \\binom{10}{8} = 5 \\times 45 = 225\n\\]\n\nSo, the number of different battalions that can be sent is 225. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\