 \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. An \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) root of unity is a complex number \\( z \\) such that \\( z^n = 1 \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can make a substitution \\( w = z^2 \\), which transforms the equation into a quadratic equation in \\( w \\):\n\\[ w^2 + w + 1 = 0. \\]\n\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\\[ w = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\n\nSo, the roots of the original equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are the square roots of \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\). These roots are:\n\\[ z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}, \\quad z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}. \\]\n\nWe can express these roots in exponential form. The complex numbers \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) are the primitive 6th roots of unity, \\( e^{2\\pi i / 6} = e^{\\pi i / 3} \\) and \\( e^{-2\\pi i / 6} = e^{-\\pi i / 3} \\), respectively. Taking the square roots of these, we get:\n\\[ z = \\pm e^{\\pi i / 6}, \\quad z = \\pm e^{-\\pi i / 6}. \\]\n\nThese roots are the 12th roots of unity because \\( e^{2k\\pi i / 12} \\) for \\( k = 1, 5, 7, 11 \\) are the roots. Therefore, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the roots of the equation z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\r\nroots = [cmath.exp(cmath.pi * 1j / 6), cmath.exp(-cmath.pi * 1j / 6), \r\n         -cmath.exp(cmath.pi * 1j / 6), -cmath.exp(-cmath.pi * 1j / 6)]\r\n\r\n# Check if these roots are 12th roots of unity\r\nn = 12\r\nis_nth_root_of_unity = all(abs(root**n - 1) < 1e-10 for root in roots)\r\n\r\nprint((is_nth_root_of_unity, n))\n```\n```output\n(True, 12)\n```\nThe Python code confirms that the roots of the equation \\( z^4 + z^2 + 