 positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( n = 6 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the roots of the polynomial z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0\r\nroots = [cmath.exp(1j * cmath.pi / 3), cmath.exp(1j * 4 * cmath.pi / 3),\r\n         cmath.exp(1j * -cmath.pi / 3), cmath.exp(1j * -4 * cmath.pi / 3)]\r\n\r\n# Function to check if a number is an nth root of unity\r\ndef is_nth_root_of_unity(z, n):\r\n    return abs(z**n - 1) < 1e-10  # Allowing a small floating point tolerance\r\n\r\n# Check the smallest n such that all roots are nth roots of unity\r\nn = 1\r\nwhile True:\r\n    if all(is_nth_root_of_unity(root, n) for root in roots):\r\n        break\r\n    n += 1\r\n\r\nprint(n)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity. The \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity are the solutions to the equation \\( z^n = 1 \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can make a substitution to simplify the polynomial. Let \\( w = z^2 \\). Then the equation becomes:\n\\[ w^2 + w + 1 = 0. \\]\n\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\\[ w = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\n\nSo, \\( z^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) or \\( z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\). The roots of these quadratic equations are the fourth roots of unity multiplied by the square roots of \\( w \\). The roots are:\n\\[ z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}, \\quad z = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}}. \\]\n\nThese roots can be expressed in terms of the com