 z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\).\n2. Determine the smallest \\( n \\) such that these roots are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity.\n\n### Step 1: Find the roots of the polynomial\n\nFirst, let\'s rewrite the polynomial \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\). We can make a substitution \\( w = z^2 \\), which transforms the equation into a quadratic equation:\n\\[ w^2 + w + 1 = 0. \\]\nThe roots of this quadratic equation are:\n\\[ w = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\nSo, we have:\n\\[ z^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\nThese values are the primitive 6th roots of unity (excluding 1). The roots of the original polynomial are therefore the square roots of these values, which are the primitive 12th roots of unity. The primitive 12th roots of unity are the roots of the polynomial \\( z^{12} - 1 = 0 \\) that are not roots of \\( z^6 - 1 = 0 \\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the smallest \\( n \\)\n\nThe roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are the primitive 12th roots of unity. Therefore, the smallest \\( n \\) such that all these roots are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( n = 12 \\).\n\nLet\'s confirm this using Python and sympy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nz = sp.symbols(\'z\')\n\n# Define the polynomial\npolynomial = z**4 + z**2 + 1\n\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\nroots = sp.solve(polynomial, z)\n\n# Check the order of the roots\nn = 12  # Since we know the roots are 12th roots of unity\nis_nth_root_of_unity = all(sp.simplify(root**n - 1) == 0 for root in roots)\n\nprint(n if is_nth_root_of_unity else "Not all roots are nth roots of unity")\n```\n```output\n12\n```\nThe smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).', "To find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) 