e angles are trisected, we have:\n- \\( \\angle BAE = \\angle EAF = \\angle FAD = x \\)\n- \\( \\angle EDA = \\angle FDC = \\angle CDA = y \\)\n\nWe are also given the following angles:\n- \\( \\angle ABC = 110^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle BCD = 100^\\circ \\)\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), we can write the equation for the sum of the angles in quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\):\n\n\\[ \\angle BAD + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDA = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSubstituting the given angles and the trisected angles, we get:\n\n\\[ 3x + 110^\\circ + 100^\\circ + 3y = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[ 3x + 3y + 210^\\circ = 360^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 3x + 3y = 150^\\circ \\]\n\\[ x + y = 50^\\circ \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the measure of angle \\( \\angle AFD \\). Notice that \\( \\angle AFD \\) is part of the straight line formed by the trisected angles \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). The angle \\( \\angle AFD \\) is the external angle to the triangle \\( \\triangle AED \\), and it is equal to the sum of the two opposite internal angles \\( \\angle EAD \\) and \\( \\angle EDA \\).\n\nSince \\( \\angle EAD = x \\) and \\( \\angle EDA = y \\), we have:\n\n\\[ \\angle AFD = x + y \\]\n\nFrom the equation \\( x + y = 50^\\circ \\), we get:\n\n\\[ \\angle AFD = 50^\\circ \\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( \\angle AFD \\) is \\( \\boxed{50} \\) degrees.", "To solve the problem, we need to find the measure of angle \\( \\angle AFD \\) in the quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\) where angles \\( \\angle BAD \\) and \\( \\angle CDA \\) are trisected.\n\n1. **Understanding the Problem:**\n   - Let \\( \\angle BAD = 3x \\) and \\( \\angle CDA = 3y \\).\n   - Since \\( \\angle BAD \\) and \\( \\angle CDA \\) are trisected, we have three equal angles at each vertex.\n   - The sum of the interior angles of quadrilateral \\( ABCD \\) is \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n   - We are given two angles in the quadrilateral: \\( \\angle ABC = 110^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle BCD