ion\ngiven_projection = (5 / 6) * b\n\n# Check if the calculated projection matches the given projection\nprint((projection, given_projection, np.allclose(projection, given_projection)))\n```\n```output\n(array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]), array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]), True)\n```\nThe calculated projection \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.8333 \\\\ -1.6667 \\\\ 0.8333 \\end{pmatrix}\\) matches the given projection \\(\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0.8333 \\\\ -1.6667 \\\\ 0.8333 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This confirms that our solution is correct.\n\nThus, the value of \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", "To find the value of \\( y \\) such that the projection of \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}\\) onto \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use the formula for the projection of vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) onto vector \\(\\mathbf{b}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe projection is given as:\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 2 \\cdot 1 + y \\cdot (-2) + (-5) \\cdot 1 = 2 - 2y - 5 = -3 - 2y\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate the dot product \\(\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 1^2 + (-2)^2 + 1^2 = 1 + 4 + 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the projection formula, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{-3 - 2y}{6} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-3 - 2y = 5\n\\]\n\\[\n-2y = 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -4\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\na = np.array([2, -4, -5])\nb = np.array([1, -2, 1])\n\n# Calculate the dot product of a and b\ndot_product_ab = np.dot(a, b)\n\n# Calculate the dot product of b with itself\ndot_product_bb = np.dot(b, b)\n\n# Calculate the projection\nprojection = (dot_product_ab / dot_product_bb) * b\n\n# The given projection\ngiven_projection = (5/6) * b\n\n# Verify if the calculated projection matches the given projection\nprint((np.allclose(projection, given_projection), projection, given_projection))\n```\n```output\n(True, array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]), array([ 0.83333333, -1.66666667,  0.83333333]))\n```\nThe calculated projection \\(\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.83333333 \\\\ -1.66666667 \\\\ 0.83333333 \\end{pmatrix}\\), which matches the given projection \\(\\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0.83333333 \\\\ -1.66666667 \\\\ 0.83333333 \\end{pmatrix}\\). This confirms that our solution is correct.\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( y \\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", "To find the value of \\( y \\), we need to use the formula for the projection of a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\):\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}.\n\\]\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ y \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{5}{6} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFirst, we compute the dot product \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \