exagon sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nHere are the steps to find \\(\\alpha\\):\n\n1. The sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\).\n2. Since the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n3. We are given two angles: \\(105^\\circ\\) and \\(110^\\circ\\). Let's denote the other angles as follows:\n   - The angle opposite to \\(105^\\circ\\) is \\(x\\).\n   - The angle opposite to \\(110^\\circ\\) is \\(y\\).\n   - The angle \\(\\alpha\\) is opposite to the remaining angle \\(z\\).\n\n4. Using the property of cyclic hexagons, we have:\n   \\[\n   x + 105^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies x = 75^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y + 110^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies y = 70^\\circ\n   \\]\n5. The sum of the interior angles of the hexagon is:\n   \\[\n   105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + \\alpha + z = 720^\\circ\n   \\]\n6. Since the opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + z = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n7. Substituting \\(z = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\\) into the sum of the interior angles, we get:\n   \\[\n   105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + \\alpha + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 720^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   540^\\circ = 720^\\circ - 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   This equation is consistent, and we can solve for \\(\\alpha\\):\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   So, \\(\\alpha\\) can be found by subtracting the known angles from \\(180^\\circ\\):\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = 180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + \\alpha))))\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + \\alpha = \\alpha\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\(\\alpha = 180^\\circ - (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ + \\alpha = \\alpha\\).\n\nLet's use Python to verify the calculation.\n```python\n# G