f of the remaining_sum\nalpha = remaining_sum / 2\n\nprint(alpha)\n```\n```output\n180.0\n```\nThe measure of the angle $\\alpha$ is $\\boxed{125^\\circ}$.", "To find the measure of the angle $\\alpha$ in the given hexagon inscribed in a circle, we need to use the properties of cyclic polygons and the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is $(6-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 720^\\circ$.\n\nGiven the angles:\n- $\\angle F = 105^\\circ$\n- $\\angle B = 110^\\circ$\n- We need to find $\\angle D = \\alpha$\n\nLet's denote the other three angles as $\\angle A$, $\\angle C$, and $\\angle E$. Since the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is $720^\\circ$, we can write:\n\\[\n\\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D + \\angle E + \\angle F = 720^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting the known angles:\n\\[\n\\angle A + 110^\\circ + \\angle C + \\alpha + \\angle E + 105^\\circ = 720^\\circ\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n\\angle A + \\angle C + \\angle E + \\alpha = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, opposite angles of the hexagon sum up to $180^\\circ$. This means:\n\\[\n\\angle A + \\angle D = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle A + \\alpha = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle B + \\angle E = 180^\\circ \\implies 110^\\circ + \\angle E = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle E = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\angle C + \\angle F = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle C + 105^\\circ = 180^\\circ \\implies \\angle C = 75^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute $\\angle C$ and $\\angle E$ back into the equation:\n\\[\n\\angle A + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + \\alpha = 505^\\circ\n\\]\nSince $\\angle A + \\alpha = 180^\\circ$, we can substitute $\\angle A = 180^\\circ - \\alpha$:\n\\[\n(180^\\circ - \\alpha) + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + \\alpha = 505^\\circ\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n325^\\circ = 505^\\circ - \\alpha\n\\]\nSolving for $\\alpha$:\n\\[\n\\alpha = 505^\\circ - 325^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nHowever, this seems incorrect because it doesn't match the expected range for a