ession):\n    n = len(expression)\n    # Generate all possible positions for parentheses\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            yield expression[:i] + ['('] + expression[i:j] + [')'] + expression[j:]\n\n# Find the minimum value\nmin_value = float('inf')\nfor parentheses in generate_parentheses(expression):\n    value = evaluate_expression(expression, parentheses)\n    if value < min_value:\n        min_value = value\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\nAttributeError: 'list' object has no attribute 'split'\n```\nThe error persists because the `eval` function expects a string, and we are providing a list. To fix this, we need to convert the list to a string before evaluating it. We can do this by using the `join` method to concatenate the elements of the list into a single string.\n\nHere is the corrected code:\n\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the expression as a list of numbers and operators\nexpression = [1, '+', 2, '+', 3, '-', 4, '+', 5, '+', 6]\n\n# Function to evaluate the expression with given parentheses\ndef evaluate_expression(expression, parentheses):\n    # Convert the list to a string\n    s = ''.join(map(str, expression))\n    # Insert parentheses\n    for i in range(len(parentheses)):\n        s = s[:i] + '(' + s[i] + ')' + s[i+1:]\n    # Evaluate the expression\n    return eval(s)\n\n# Generate all possible ways to add parentheses\ndef generate_parentheses(expression):\n    n = len(expression)\n    # Generate all possible positions for parentheses\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i+1, n):\n            yield expression[:i] + ['('] + expression[i:j] + [')'] + expression[j:]\n\n# Find the minimum value\nmin_value = float('inf')\nfor parentheses in generate_parentheses(expression):\n    value = evaluate_expression(parentheses, expression)\n    if value < min_value:\n        min_value = value\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\n-2\n```\n\nReach max function call limit.", 'To solve this problem,