the maximum value\nequation = sp.Eq(f_at_2, 4)\n\n# Solve for a\na_value = sp.solve(equation, a)[0]\nprint(a_value)\n```\n```output\n-4\n```\nThe value of \\(a\\) such that the function \\(f(x) = x^2 + 2x + a\\) has a maximum value of 4 on the interval \\([-3, 2]\\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).\n\nA cat has nine lives, each life can be divided into 4 parts. A dog has 3 parts less per life than a cat. A mouse has 7 parts more per life than a dog. How many total parts of lives does a mouse have?\nTo solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the number of parts per life for a dog.\n2. Determine the number of parts per life for a mouse.\n3. Calculate the total number of parts for a mouse's nine lives.\n\nWe know that:\n- A cat has 4 parts per life.\n- A dog has 3 parts less per life than a cat.\n- A mouse has 7 parts more per life than a dog.\n\nLet's start by calculating the number of parts per life for a dog and a mouse. Then we will calculate the total number of parts for a mouse's nine lives.\n\n1. Number of parts per life for a dog:\n\\[ \\text{parts\\_per\\_life\\_dog} = \\text{parts\\_per\\_life\\_cat} - 3 = 4 - 3 = 1 \\]\n\n2. Number of parts per life for a mouse:\n\\[ \\text{parts\\_per\\_life\\_mouse} = \\text{parts\\_per\\_life\\_dog} + 7 = 1 + 7 = 8 \\]\n\n3. Total number of parts for a mouse's nine lives:\n\\[ \\text{total\\_parts\\_mouse} = 9 \\times \\text{parts\\_per\\_life\\_mouse} = 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\]\n\nNow let's write the Python code to verify this calculation.\n```python\n# Given values\nparts_per_life_cat = 4\nparts_per_life_dog = parts_per_life_cat - 3\nparts_per_life_mouse = parts_per_life_dog + 7\nnumber_of_lives_mouse = 9\n\n# Total parts for a mouse's nine lives\ntotal_parts_mouse = number_of_lives_mouse * parts_per_life_mouse\nprint(total_parts_mouse)\n```\n```output\n72\n```\nThe total number of parts of lives that a mouse has is \\(\\boxed{72}\\).\n\nGiven that the sum of the first $n$ terms of the sequence $\\{a_{n}\\}$