ow, we can find \\(D\\):\n\n\\[D = 2A = 2 \\times 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\\]\n\nSo, the measure of the largest angle is \\(120^\\circ\\). Let's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the angles\nA = 60  # smallest angle\nB = 90  # right angle\nC = 90  # right angle\nD = 2 * A  # largest angle\n\n# Sum of angles in a quadrilateral\nsum_of_angles = A + B + C + D\n\n# Check if the sum of angles is 360 degrees\nprint((sum_of_angles, D))\n```\n```output\n(360, 120)\n```\nThe sum of the angles is indeed \\(360^\\circ\\), confirming that our calculations are correct. The measure of the largest angle is \\(\\boxed{120}\\) degrees.", "To solve this problem, let's define the angles of the quadrilateral as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). We know the following:\n\n1. \\(A\\) is the largest angle.\n2. \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) are both right angles, so \\(B = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(C = 90^\\circ\\).\n3. \\(A = 2D\\), where \\(D\\) is the smallest angle.\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always \\(360^\\circ\\), we can write the equation:\n\\[A + B + C + D = 360^\\circ\\]\nSubstituting the known values, we get:\n\\[A + 90^\\circ + 90^\\circ + D = 360^\\circ\\]\n\\[A + D + 180^\\circ = 360^\\circ\\]\n\\[A + D = 180^\\circ\\]\nSince \\(A = 2D\\), we can substitute \\(A\\) in the equation:\n\\[2D + D = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[3D = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[D = 60^\\circ\\]\nNow, we can find \\(A\\):\n\\[A = 2D = 2 \\times 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ\\]\nSo, the largest angle \\(A\\) is \\(120^\\circ\\). Let's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the smallest angle D\nD = 180 / 3  # Since 3D = 180, D = 180 / 3\n\n# Calculate the largest angle A\nA = 2 * D\n\n# Output the largest angle A\nprint(A)\n```\n```output\n120.0\n```\nThe measure of the largest angle in the convex quadrilateral is \\(\\boxed{120}\\) degrees.", "Let's denote the smallest angle by \\( \\theta \\). The largest angle is then \\( 2\\theta \\).