{4} = 144,\n\\]\n\\[\n64 \\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right)^3 = 64 \\cdot \\frac{27}{8} = 216.\n\\]\n\nThis shows that \\(n = 64\\) satisfies all conditions. Since \\(n\\) must be at least 64 and we have verified that \\(n = 64\\) works, there are no other possible values for \\(n\\).\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\(n\\) is \\(64\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]", 'To solve the problem of dividing \\(413_5\\) by \\(2_5\\) and expressing the result in base 5, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the base 5 numbers to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the result back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s break it down step by step.\n\n1. Convert \\(413_5\\) to base 10.\n\\[413_5 = 4 \\cdot 5^2 + 1 \\cdot 5^1 + 3 \\cdot 5^0 = 4 \\cdot 25 + 1 \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot 1 = 100 + 5 + 3 = 108_{10}\\]\n\n2. Convert \\(2_5\\) to base 10.\n\\[2_5 = 2 \\cdot 5^0 = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2_{10}\\]\n\n3. Perform the division in base 10.\n\\[108_{10} \\div 2_{10} = 54_{10}\\]\n\n4. Convert \\(54_{10}\\) back to base 5.\nTo convert a number from base 10 to base 5, we repeatedly divide the number by 5 and keep track of the remainders.\n\n\\[54 \\div 5 = 10 \\text{ remainder } 4\\]\n\\[10 \\div 5 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 0\\]\n\\[2 \\div 5 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 2\\]\n\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get \\(204_5\\).\n\nSo, the quotient \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(204_5\\).\n\nLet\'s verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to convert a base 10 number to base 5\ndef to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % 5))\n        n //= 5\n    return \'\'.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 413 base 5 to base 10\nnum_base_10 = 4 * 25 + 1 * 5 + 3 * 1\n\n# Convert 2 base 5 to base 10\ndivisor_base_10 = 2\n\n# Perform the division in base 10\nquotient_base_10 = num_base_10 // divisor_base_10\n\n# Convert the quotient back to base 5\nquotient_base_5 = to_base_5(quotient_base_10)\nprint(quotient_base_5)\n```\n```output\n204\n```\nThe quotient of \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(\\boxed{204_5}\\).', 'To solve the problem of dividing $413_5$ by $2_5$ and expressing the result in base 5, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the base 5 numbers to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the quotient back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s start by converting $413_5$ and $2_5$ to base 10.\n\nThe number $413_5$ in base 10 is:\n\\[ 4 \\cdot 5^2 + 1 \\cdot 5^1 + 3 \\cdot 5^0 = 4 \\cdot 25 + 1 \\cdot 5 + 3 \\cdot 1 = 100 + 5 + 3 = 108_{10} \\]\n\nThe number $2_5$ in base 10 is:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot 5^0 = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2_{10} \\]\n\nNow, we perform the division in base 10:\n\\[ 108 \\div 2 = 54 \\]\n\nNext, we convert the quotient $54_{10}$ back to base 5. To do this, we repeatedly divide the number by 5 and keep track of the remainders.\n\n\\[ 54 \\div 5 = 10 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n\\[ 10 \\div 5 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\div 5 = 0 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\n\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get the base 5 representation:\n\\[ 54_{10} = 204_5 \\]\n\nSo, the quotient of $413_5 \\div 2_5$ in base 5 is $204_5$.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to convert a base 10 number to base 5\ndef to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % 5))\n        n //= 5\n    return \'\'.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 413_5 and 2_5 to base 10\nnum1_base_10 = 4 * 25 + 1 * 5 + 3 * 1\nnum2_base_10 = 2\n\n# Perform the division in base 10\nquotient_base_10 = num1_base_10 // num2_base_10\n\n# Convert the quotient back to base 5\nquotient_base_5 = to_base_5(quotient_base_10)\nprint(quotient_base_5)\n```\n```output\n204\n```\nThe quotient of $413_5 \\div 2_5$ in base 5 is $\\boxed{204_5}$.', 'To solve the problem of dividing \\(413_5\\) by \\(2_5\\) and expressing the quotient in base 5, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the base 5 numbers to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the quotient back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s break it down:\n\n1. Convert \\(413_5\\) to base 10:\n   \\[\n   413_5 = 4 \\cdot 5^2 + 1 \\cdot 5^1 + 3 \\cdot 5^0 = 4 \\cdot 25 + 1 \\cdot 5 + 3 = 100 + 5 + 3 = 108_{10}\n   \\]\n\n2. Convert \\(2_5\\) to base 10:\n   \\[\n   2_5 = 2 \\cdot 5^0 = 2_{10}\n   \\]\n\n3. Perform the division in base 10:\n   \\[\n   108_{10} \\div 2_{10} = 54_{10}\n   \\]\n\n4. Convert \\(54_{10}\\) back to base 5. To do this, we repeatedly divide the number by 5 and keep track of the remainders.\n\nLet\'s perform the conversion:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n54 \\div 5 &= 10 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\\\\n10 \\div 5 &= 2 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\\\\n2 \\div 5 &= 0 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get \\(204_5\\).\n\nSo, the quotient of \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(204_5\\).\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to convert a base 10 number to base 5\ndef to_base_5(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return "0"\n    digits = []\n    while n:\n        digits.append(int(n % 5))\n        n //= 5\n    return \'\'.join(str(x) for x in digits[::-1])\n\n# Convert 413_5 to base 10\nnum_base_10 = 4 * 25 + 1 * 5 + 3\n\n# Convert 2_5 to base 10\ndivisor_base_10 = 2\n\n# Perform the division in base 10\nquotient_base_10 = num_base_10 // divisor_base_10\n\n# Convert the quotient back to base 5\nquotient_base_5 = to_base_5(quotient_base_10)\nprint(quotient_base_5)\n```\n```output\n204\n```\nThe quotient of \\(413_5 \\div 2_5\\) in base 5 is \\(\\boxed{204_5}\\).', 'To solve the problem of dividing \\(413_5\\) by \\(2_5\\) and expressing the result in base 5, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the numbers from base 5 to base 10.\n2. Perform the division in base 10.\n3. Convert the result back to base 5.\n\nLet\'s start by converting \\(413_5\\) and \\(2_5\\) to base 