oks remaining.\n3. The bottom shelf initially has 6 science books. After removing 2 books, the bottom shelf has \\(6 - 2 = 4\\) books remaining.\n\nThe total number of books remaining on all three shelves is \\(6 + 8 + 4 = 18\\). The number of math books remaining on the middle shelf is 8.\n\nThe fraction of the remaining books that are math books is \\(\\frac{8}{18}\\). We can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 2. So, \\(\\frac{8}{18} = \\frac{4}{9}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{9}}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Initial number of books on each shelf\ntop_shelf_initial = 8\nmiddle_shelf_initial = 10\nbottom_shelf_initial = 6\n\n# Books removed from each shelf\nbooks_removed_per_shelf = 2\n\n# Books remaining on each shelf after removal\ntop_shelf_remaining = top_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\nmiddle_shelf_remaining = middle_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\nbottom_shelf_remaining = bottom_shelf_initial - books_removed_per_shelf\n\n# Total number of books remaining\ntotal_books_remaining = top_shelf_remaining + middle_shelf_remaining + bottom_shelf_remaining\n\n# Number of math books remaining (only on the middle shelf)\nmath_books_remaining = middle_shelf_remaining\n\n# Fraction of remaining books that are math books\nfraction_math_books = math_books_remaining / total_books_remaining\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nsimplified_fraction = Fraction(math_books_remaining, total_books_remaining)\n\nprint(simplified_fraction)\n```", 'The total number of books remaining on the three shelves after two books are taken off each shelf is 3 * 2 = 6 books. The number of math books remaining on the middle shelf is 10 - 2 = 8 books. Therefore, the fraction of the books remaining on the three shelves that are math books is 8 / 6 = 4 / 3. \\boxed{4 / 3}', "The top shelf has 8 mystery books, the middle shelf has 10 math