ctors of 50 and then filter out the two-digit factors. The factors of 50 are:\n- \\(1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\\)\n\nAmong these, the two-digit factors are \\(10, 25, 50\\). Therefore, the number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is 3.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to find the GCD of two numbers\ndef gcd(a, b):\n    return math.gcd(a, b)\n\n# Function to find all factors of a number\ndef factors(n):\n    result = set()\n    for i in range(1, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            result.add(i)\n            result.add(n // i)\n    return result\n\n# Find the GCD of 100 and 150\ngcd_100_150 = gcd(100, 150)\n\n# Find all factors of the GCD\nall_factors = factors(gcd_100_150)\n\n# Filter out the two-digit factors\ntwo_digit_factors = [factor for factor in all_factors if 10 <= factor < 100]\n\n# The number of two-digit factors\nnum_two_digit_factors = len(two_digit_factors)\nprint(num_two_digit_factors)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe number of positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).", "To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we need to find the common factors of 100 and 150 that are two-digit numbers. The steps are as follows:\n\n1. Find the prime factorization of 100 and 150.\n2. Determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n3. List all the factors of the GCD.\n4. Identify which of these factors are two-digit numbers.\n\nLet's start by finding the prime factorization of 100 and 150:\n\n- \\(100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\\)\n\nThe GCD of 100 and 150 is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor appearing in the factorizations of both numbers:\n\n- \\( \\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2 \\times 5^2 = 50 \\)\n\nNext, we list all the factors of 50:\n\n- The factors of 50 are: 1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50.\n\nAmong these factors, the two-digit numbers are 1