b = c\\).\n\nTherefore, the set of all possible values of \\(E\\) is \\([\\frac{5}{2}, \\infty)\\).\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\na, b, c = sp.symbols(\'a b c\', positive=True)\n\n# Define the expression\nE = c/a + a/(b + c) + b/c\n\n# Find the critical points by taking the partial derivatives and setting them to zero\npartial_a = sp.diff(E, a)\npartial_b = sp.diff(E, b)\npartial_c = sp.diff(E, c)\n\n# Solve the system of equations\ncritical_points = sp.solve([partial_a, partial_b, partial_c], (a, b, c), dict=True)\n\n# Evaluate the expression at the critical points\nmin_value = float(\'inf\')\nfor point in critical_points:\n    value = E.subs(point)\n    if value < min_value:\n        min_value = value\n\n# Check the behavior as a, b, or c approach zero or infinity\nlimit_a_small = sp.limit(E, a, 0)\nlimit_c_small = sp.limit(E, c, 0)\nlimit_b_small = sp.limit(E, b, 0)\nlimit_a_large = sp.limit(E, a, sp.oo)\nlimit_b_large = sp.limit(E, b, sp.oo)\nlimit_c_large = sp.limit(E, c, sp.oo)\n\n# Print the results\nprint(f"Critical points: {critical_points}")\nprint(f"Minimum value: {min_value}")\nprint(f"Limit as a approaches 0: {limit_a_small}")\nprint(f"Limit as c approaches 0: {limit_c_small}")\nprint(f"Limit as b approaches 0: {limit_b_small}")\nprint(f"Limit as a approaches infinity: {limit_a_large}")\nprint(f"Limit as b approaches infinity: {limit_b_large}")\nprint(f"Limit as c approaches infinity: {limit_c_large}")\n```\n```output\ntimeout_decorator.timeout_decorator.TimeoutError: \'Timed Out\'\n```\nIt seems that the computation took too long to execute. Let\'s simplify our approach by focusing on the key points and using some mathematical reasoning instead of relying on symbolic computation for the entire process.\n\nWe have already established that the expression \\(E = \\frac{c}{a} + \\frac{a}{b + c} + \\frac{b}{c}\\) can be arbitrarily large by setting \\(a\\) to be very small compared 