ls and 2 boys in a row where the 2 boys must sit next to each other, we can treat the two boys as a single "block". This reduces the problem to arranging 7 "blocks" (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys).\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. Treat the two boys as a single block. This means we have 7 blocks to arrange (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys).\n2. The number of ways to arrange 7 blocks is \\(7!\\) (7 factorial).\n3. Within the block of 2 boys, the boys can switch places, so there are \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways to arrange the boys within their block.\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 blocks\nways_to_arrange_blocks = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block\nways_to_arrange_boys = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = ways_to_arrange_blocks * ways_to_arrange_boys\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, where the 2 boys must sit next to each other, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can treat the two boys as a single "block" or "unit" since they must sit next to each other. This means we are essentially arranging 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys) in a row.\n\n1. First, we calculate the number of ways to arrange these 7 units. This is simply 7! (7 factorial).\n2. Next, we need to consider the number of ways to arrange the two boys within their block. There are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange the two boys.\n\nSo, the total number of ways to arrange the 6 girls and 2 boys, with the boys always sitting next to each other, is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys)\nways_to_arrange_units = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block\nways_to_arrange_boys = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = ways_to_arrange_units * ways_to_arrange_boys\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, with the 2 boys always sitting next to each other, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can treat the two boys as a single "block" or "unit" since they must sit next to each other. This means we are essentially arranging 7 units (the block of 2 boys and the 6 girls) in a row.\n\n1. First, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange these 7 units. This is simply \\(7!\\) (7 factorial), which is the number of permutations of 7 distinct items.\n2. Next, we need to consider the number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block. There are \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block (i.e., boy 1 can be on the left and boy 2 on the right, or vice versa).\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 units (the block of 2 boys and the 6 girls)\narrangements_of_units = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange the 2 boys within their block\narrangements_of_boys = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = arrangements_of_units * arrangements_of_boys\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row if the 2 boys insist on sitting next to each other is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can treat the two boys as a single "block" or "unit" since they must sit next to each other. This means we are effectively arranging 7 units (6 girls + 1 block of 2 boys) in a row. There are 7! (7 factorial) ways to arrange these 7 units. Additionally, within the block of 2 boys, there are 2! (2 factorial) ways to arrange the two boys.\n\nSo, the total number of ways to arrange the 6 girls and 2 boys, with the boys sitting next to each other, is given by:\n\\[ 7! \\times 2! \\]\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate 7! (7 factorial)\nfactorial_7 = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Calculate 2! (2 factorial)\nfactorial_2 = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = factorial_7 * factorial_2\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, with the 2 boys sitting next to each other, is \\(\\boxed{10080}\\).', 'To solve this problem, we can think of the two boys as a single "block" or "super boy". This reduces the problem to arranging 7 entities (6 girls + 1 "super boy"). The "super boy" can be in any of these 7 positions, and within the "super boy", the two boys can switch places. So, we need to calculate the number of ways to arrange these 7 entities and then multiply by the number of ways to arrange the two boys within their block.\n\nHere\'s the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Treat the two boys as a single "super boy". This gives us 7 entities to arrange (6 girls + 1 "super boy").\n2. The number of ways to arrange 7 entities is \\(7!\\) (7 factorial).\n3. Within the "super boy", the two boys can switch places, so there are \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways to arrange the two boys.\n\nSo, the total number of arrangements is \\(7! \\times 2!\\).\n\nLet\'s calculate this using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Number of ways to arrange 7 entities (6 girls + 1 "super boy")\narrangements_of_entities = math.factorial(7)\n\n# Number of ways to arrange the two boys within their block\narrangements_of_boys = math.factorial(2)\n\n# Total number of arrangements\ntotal_arrangements = arrangements_of_entities * arrangements_of_boys\nprint(total_arrangements)\n```\n```output\n10080\n```\nThe total number of ways to arrange 6 girls and 2 boys in a row, with the condition that the 2 boys must sit next to e