\sum_{i=1}^{365} X_i \\). By linearity of expectation, the expected value of \\( X \\) is:\n\\[ E[X] = E\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^{365} X_i\\right] = \\sum_{i=1}^{365} E[X_i] = 365 \\cdot \\frac{1}{365} = 1. \\]\n\nSo, the expected number of days in the year that have at least one birthday celebration is \\( \\boxed{1} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code simulation to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Number of students\nn = 500\n# Number of days in a year\nd = 365\n# Probability that at least one student has a birthday on a given day\np = 1 / d\n\n# Number of simulations\nnum_simulations = 100000\n\n# Simulate the number of days with at least one birthday celebration\ndef simulate_birthday_celebrations(n, d, p, num_simulations):\n    results = []\n    for _ in range(num_simulations):\n        # Generate random birthdays for each student\n        birthdays = np.random.choice(d, n, replace=True)\n        # Count the number of unique birthdays\n        unique_birthdays = np.unique(birthdays)\n        # The number of days with at least one birthday celebration\n        num_days_with_celebration = len(unique_birthdays)\n        results.append(num_days_with_celebration)\n    return np.mean(results)\n\n# Run the simulation\nexpected_days_with_celebration = simulate_birthday_celebrations(n, d, p, num_simulations)\nprint(expected_days_with_celebration)\n```\n\nHuman: Consider a set of integers from 1 to 100. Define a function $f: \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 100\\} \\to \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 100\\}$ such that for any integer $x$ in this set, $f(x) = 3x + 5$ if $x$ is less than 34, and $f(x) = 2x - 10$ otherwise. Determine the number of integers $x$ in this set for which $f(f(x)) = x$.\n\nAssistant: To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of integers \\( x \\) in the set \\(\\{1, 2, \\ldots, 100\\}\\) for which \\( f(f(x)) = x \\). We will break this down into manageable steps:\n\n1. Define the function \\( f(x) \\) based on the given 