^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta}).\n    \\]\n    Simplifying this, we get:\n    \\[\n    (1 + e^{i\\theta})(1 + e^{-i\\theta}) = 1 + e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta} + 1 = 2 + 2\\cos(\\theta) = 2 + 2\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) = 2 + 2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} = 3.\n    \\]\n    Similarly,\n    \\[\n    (-1 + e^{i\\theta})(-1 + e^{-i\\theta}) = 1 - e^{i\\theta} - e^{-i\\theta} + 1 = 2 - 2\\cos(\\theta) = 2 - 2\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) = 2 - 2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} = 1.\n    \\]\n    And,\n    \\[\n    e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta} = 2\\cos(\\theta) = 2\\cos(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) = 2\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} = 1.\n    \\]\n    So the product is:\n    \\[\n    (1 - 1)(1 + e^{i\\theta})(1 + e^{-i\\theta})(-1 + e^{i\\theta})(-1 + e^{-i\\theta})(e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta}) = 0.\n    \\]\n    This configuration also doesn't work because it results in zero.\n\n11. **Final Answer:**\n    The maximum value of \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\) is 16, which occurs when \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -1\\), \\(c = i\\), \\(d = -i\\).\n\nLet's write the Python code to confirm this result.\n```python\nimport cmath\n\n# Define the complex numbers a, b, c, d\na = 1\nb = -1\nc = cmath.exp(1j * cmath.pi / 3)\nd = cmath.exp(-1j * cmath.pi / 3)\n\n# Calculate the product (a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)\nproduct = (a + b) * (a + c) * (a + d) * (b + c) * (b + d) * (c + d)\n\n# Calculate the absolute value of the product\nabs_product = abs(product)\n\nprint(abs_product)\n```\n```output\n0.0\n```\nIt seems that the configuration \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -1\\), \\(c = e^{i\\pi/3}\\), \\(d = e^{-i\\pi/3}\\) also results in a product of zero. This suggests that we need to consider another configuration.\n\nLet's consider the configuration \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -1\\), \\(c = i\\), \\(d = -i\\). This configuration satisfies the conditions and should give us the maximum value.\n\nLet's calculate the product for this configuration again:\n\\[\n(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d) = (1 - 1)(1 + i)(1 - i)(-1 + i)(-1 - i)(