(1, 4), (2, 1), (3, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4), (3, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)}\n\n# Count the number of shaded squares\nshaded_count = 0\nfor first in first_spin:\n    for second in second_spin:\n        column, row = get_column_and_row(first, second)\n        if (column, row) in shaded_squares:\n            shaded_count += 1\n\n# Total possible outcomes\ntotal_outcomes = len(first_spin) * len(second_spin)\n\n# Probability\nprobability = sp.Rational(shaded_count, total_outcomes)\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n2/9\n```\nThe probability that the checker is placed on a shaded square of the checkerboard is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that the checker is placed on a shaded square of the checkerboard. The steps are as follows:\n\n1. Identify the possible outcomes for each spin of the wheel.\n2. Determine the remainders when the numbers are divided by 4 and 5.\n3. Map these remainders to the columns and rows of the checkerboard.\n4. Identify which squares are shaded on the checkerboard.\n5. Calculate the probability that the checker lands on a shaded square.\n\nFirst, let's list the possible outcomes for each spin of the wheel:\n- The first spin can result in 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, or 9.\n- The second spin can result in 1, 2, 3, 4, or 9.\n\nNext, we need to determine the remainders when these numbers are divided by 4 and 5:\n- For the first spin, the remainders when divided by 4 are:\n  - \\(1 \\div 4 = 1\\) (remainder 1)\n  - \\(2 \\div 4 = 0\\) (remainder 2)\n  - \\(3 \\div 4 = 0\\) (remainder 3)\n  - \\(4 \\div 4 = 1\\) (remainder 0)\n  - \\(6 \\div 4 = 1\\) (remainder 2)\n  - \\(9 \\div 4 = 2\\) (remainder 1)\n  So the possible remainders are 0, 1, and 2.\n- For the second spin, the remainders when divided by 5 are:\n  - \\(1 \\div 5 = 0\\) (remainder 1)\n  - \\(2 \\div 5 = 0\\) (remainder 2)\n  - \\(3 \\div 5 = 0\\) (remainder 3)\n  - \\(4 \\div 5 = 0\\) (remainder 4)\n  - \\(9 \\div 5 = 1\\) (remainder 4)\n  So the possible remainders are 1, 2, 3, and 4.\n\nNow we need to map these remainders to the columns and rows of the checkerboard:\n- The remainders 0, 1, and 2 for the first spin correspond to columns 3, 1, and 2, respectively.\n- The remainders 1, 2, 3, and 4 for the second spin correspond to rows 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively.\n\nFinally, we need to identify which squares are shaded on the checkerboard. The shaded squares are those in the 1st and 3rd columns and the 2nd and 4th rows. So the shaded squares are:\n- Column 1, Row 2\n- Column 1, Row 4\n- Column 2, Row 2\n- Column 2, Row 4\n- Column 3, Row 2\n- Column 3, Row 4\n\nWe can now calculate the probability that the checker lands on a shaded square. There are a total of \\(6 \\times 5 = 30\\) possible outcomes. The number of favorable outcomes is the number of ways to land on a shaded square. We can count these outcomes as follows:\n- For column 1 (remainder 2), there are 2 favorable rows (2 and 4).\n- For column 2 (remainder 1), there are 2 favorable rows (2 and 4).\n- For column 3 (remainder 0), there are 2 favorable rows (2 and 4).\n\nSo there are \\(2 + 2 + 2 = 6\\) favorable outcomes for each column, and since there are 3 columns, there are \\(6 \\times 3 = 18\\) favorable outcomes in total.\n\nThe probability is therefore \\(\\frac{18}{30} = \\frac{3}{5}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the possible outcomes for each spin of the wheel\nfirst_spin = [1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9]\nsecond_spin = [1, 2, 3, 4, 9]\n\n# Calculate the remainders when divided by 4 and 5\nfirst_remainders = [num % 4 for num in first_spin]\nsecond_remainders = [num % 5 for num in second_spin]\n\n# Map the remainders to columns and rows\ncolumns = [3 if r == 0 else 1 if r == 1 else 2 for r in first_remainders]\nrows = [1 if r == 1 else 2 if r == 2 else 3 if r == 3 else 4 for r in second_remainders]\n\n# Identify the shaded squares\nshaded_squares = [(col, row) for col, row in zip(columns, rows) if (col in [1, 3] and