ce of odd numbers can be expressed as \\(2n - 1\\). This is because the sequence starts at 1 (which is \\(2 \\cdot 1 - 1\\)), and each subsequent term increases by 2.\n\nFor the 2003rd term, we can substitute \\(n = 2003\\) into the formula \\(2n - 1\\):\n\n\\[ \\text{2003rd term} = 2 \\cdot 2003 - 1 \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Calculate the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers\nn = 2003\nterm_2003 = 2 * n - 1\nprint(term_2003)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\), we can observe that the \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by the formula \\(2n - 1\\). This is because the sequence starts at 1 and increases by 2 each time.\n\nFor the 2003rd term, we substitute \\(n = 2003\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[a_{2003} = 2 \\cdot 2003 - 1 = 4006 - 1 = 4005\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Calculate the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers\nn = 2003\nterm_2003 = 2 * n - 1\nprint(term_2003)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\), we need to recognize the pattern of the sequence. The \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence of odd numbers can be given by the formula \\(2n - 1\\).\n\nFor the 2003rd term, we substitute \\(n = 2003\\) into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{2003rd term} = 2 \\cdot 2003 - 1 = 4006 - 1 = 4005 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Calculate the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers\nn = 2003\nterm = 2 * n - 1\nprint(term)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers, we can use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence. The sequence of odd numbers is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1 = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\).\n\nThe formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1) \\cdot d \\]\n\nFor the 2003rd term (\\(n = 2003\\)):\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + (2003-1) \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 2002 \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 4004 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 4005 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the first term and the common difference\na1 = 1\nd = 2\n\n# Calculate the 2003rd term\nn = 2003\na_n = a1 + (n - 1) * d\nprint(a_n)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\dots\\), we need to recognize the pattern in the sequence. The \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence of odd numbers can be given by the formula \\(2n - 1\\). This is because the first term is \\(1 = 2 \\cdot 1 - 1\\), the second term is \\(3 = 2 \\cdot 2 - 1\\), the third term is \\(5 = 2 \\cdot 3 - 1\\), and so on.\n\nSo, the 2003rd term of the sequence is \\(2 \\cdot 2003 - 1 = 4006 - 1 = 4005\\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to find the nth term of the sequence of odd numbers\ndef nth_odd_number(n):\n    return 2 * n - 1\n\n# Find the 2003rd term\nterm_2003 = nth_odd_number(2003)\nprint(term_2003)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\dots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "The sequence of odd numbers is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1 = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\). The general formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1) \\cdot d \\]\n\nTo find the 2003rd term, we substitute \\(n = 2003\\), \\(a_1 = 1\\), and \\(d = 2\\) into the formula:\n\