-th term of an arithmetic progression is given by:\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term, \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nFor the 2003rd term, we substitute \\(a = 1\\), \\(d = 2\\), and \\(n = 2003\\) into the formula:\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + (2003-1) \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 2002 \\cdot 2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 1 + 4004 \\]\n\\[ a_{2003} = 4005 \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# First term of the arithmetic progression\na = 1\n# Common difference\nd = 2\n# Term number\nn = 2003\n\n# Calculate the 2003rd term\na_n = a + (n - 1) * d\nprint(a_n)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\), we can observe that the \\(n\\)-th term of this sequence is given by the formula \\(2n - 1\\). This is because the sequence of odd numbers is an arithmetic sequence with the first term \\(a_1 = 1\\) and a common difference \\(d = 2\\). The formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is:\n\n\\[a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\\]\n\nFor the sequence of odd numbers, this becomes:\n\n\\[a_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2 = 2n - 1\\]\n\nSo, the 2003rd term of the sequence is:\n\n\\[a_{2003} = 2 \\cdot 2003 - 1 = 4006 - 1 = 4005\\]\n\nLet's confirm this by writing a Python code to compute the 2003rd term.\n```python\n# Calculate the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers\nn = 2003\nterm = 2 * n - 1\nprint(term)\n```\n```output\n4005\n```\nThe 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers \\(1, 3, 5, 7, \\ldots\\) is \\(\\boxed{4005}\\).", "To find the 2003rd term of the sequence of odd numbers, we can use the formula for the nth term of an arithmetic sequence. The sequence of odd numbers is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a_1\\) is 1 and the common difference \\(d\\) is 2.\n\nThe formula for the nth term o