gles:**\n   - Since \\( \\angle JOH \\) and \\( \\angle JNH \\) are both \\(90^\\circ\\), the sum of these angles is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n   - The angle \\( \\angle J \\) is the remaining angle in the quadrilateral \\(JOHN\\), and since the sum of the angles in any quadrilateral is \\(360^\\circ\\), we have:\n     \\[\n     m\\angle J + m\\angle H + m\\angle JOH + m\\angle JNH = 360^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(m\\angle JOH = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(m\\angle JNH = 90^\\circ\\), we get:\n     \\[\n     m\\angle J + m\\angle H + 90^\\circ + 90^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     m\\angle J + m\\angle H + 180^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Therefore:\n     \\[\n     m\\angle J + m\\angle H = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n\nThe number of degrees in the sum of \\(m\\angle J\\) and \\(m\\angle H\\) is \\(\\boxed{180}\\).', "To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of tangents and circles. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Understand the Geometry:**\n   - \\(HO\\) and \\(HN\\) are tangent to the circle at points \\(O\\) and \\(N\\), respectively.\n   - The tangent segments from a point outside the circle to the points of tangency are equal. So, \\(HO = HN\\).\n   - The angle between a tangent and a chord through the point of contact is equal to the angle in the alternate segment. In this case, \\(\\angle HNO = \\angle HON\\).\n\n2. **Properties of Tangents:**\n   - The radius of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at the point of tangency. So, \\(JO \\perp HO\\) and \\(JN \\perp HN\\).\n   - This means \\(\\angle JOH = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle JNH = 90^\\circ\\).\n\n3. **Sum of Angles in Quadrilateral:**\n   - The sum of the angles in quadrilateral \\(JONH\\) is \\(360^\\circ\\).\n   - We know \\(\\angle JOH = 90^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle JNH = 90^\\circ\\).\n   - Let \\(\\angle J = \\angle OJN\\) and \\(\\angle H = \\angle HNO\\).\n   - Since \\(HO = HN\\), \\(\\angle HNO = \\angle HON\\).\n   - Therefore,