ion, we take the reciprocal of this fractional part:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 3}\n   \\]\n   We rationalize the denominator by multiplying the numerator and the denominator by $\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{4} + \\sqrt[3]{2} - 3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1}{\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1}{(\\sqrt[3]{4})^3 + (\\sqrt[3]{2})^3 - 3(\\sqrt[3]{4} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} \\cdot 1) - 27} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1}{4 + 2 - 6 - 27} = \\frac{\\sqrt[3]{16} - \\sqrt[3]{8} + \\sqrt[3]{4} - 1}{-27}\n   \\]\n   This process can be continued, but we can see that the integer part of this expression will be $1$. We can repeat the process to find the next terms, but for the purpose of this problem, we only need to find the value of $b$.\n\nFrom the steps above, we can see that the continued fraction representation of $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ starts as:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1 + \\cfrac{1}{4 + \\cfrac{1}{\\text{some expression}}}\n\\]\nSo, the value of $b$ is $4$.\n\nLet's confirm this by implementing the steps in Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import Rational, cbrt, nsimplify\n\n# Define the cube root of 2\ncube_root_2 = cbrt(2)\n\n# Find the integer part of cube_root_2\na = int(cube_root_2)\n\n# Find the fractional part\nfractional_part = cube_root_2 - a\n\n# Find the reciprocal of the fractional part\nreciprocal_fractional_part = 1 / fractional_part\n\n# Find the integer part of the reciprocal of the fractional part\nb = int(reciprocal_fractional_part)\n\nprint(b)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nIt appears there was an error in the initial reasoning or the implementation. Let's correct the process and re-evaluate the continued fraction representation of $\\sqrt[3]{2}$.\n\nWe need to carefully rationalize the denominators and correctly identify the integer parts at each step. Let's re-evalu