ct of these integers is:\n\n\\[ (n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1) = 120 \\]\n\nThe mean of these three integers is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{(n-1) + n + (n+1)}{3} = \\frac{3n}{3} = n \\]\n\nAccording to the problem, the product of the three integers divided by the mean of the three integers is 24. So we have:\n\n\\[ \\frac{(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1)}{n} = 24 \\]\n\nSince we already know that the product of the three integers is 120, we can substitute this into the equation:\n\n\\[ \\frac{120}{n} = 24 \\]\n\nFrom this equation, we can solve for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[ 120 = 24n \\]\n\\[ n = \\frac{120}{24} = 5 \\]\n\nSo the three consecutive integers are \\( 4 \\), \\( 5 \\), and \\( 6 \\). The largest of these three integers is \\( 6 \\).\n\nLet\'s verify this by calculating the product and the mean:\n\n\\[ 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 = 120 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{4 + 5 + 6}{3} = 5 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{120}{5} = 24 \\]\n\nEverything checks out, so the largest of the three consecutive integers is \\( 6 \\).\n\nLet\'s write the Python code to confirm this solution.\n```python\n# Define the function to find the three consecutive integers\ndef find_consecutive_integers(product):\n    # Iterate over possible values of n\n    for n in range(1, 100):  # We can limit the range to a reasonable number\n        if (n-1) * n * (n+1) == product:\n            return n-1, n, n+1\n    return None\n\n# Given product of three consecutive integers\nproduct = 120\n\n# Find the three consecutive integers\nconsecutive_integers = find_consecutive_integers(product)\nprint((consecutive_integers, max(consecutive_integers) if consecutive_integers else "No solution"))\n```\n```output\n((4, 5, 6), 6)\n```\nThe three consecutive integers are \\(4\\), \\(5\\), and \\(6\\), and the largest of these integers is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).', "Let's denote the three consecutive integers as \\( n-1 \\), \\( n \\), and \\( n+1 \\).\n\nFirst, we know the product of these three integers is 120:\n\\[\n(n-1) \\cdot n \\cdot (n+1