nits digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFrom the above calculations, we can see that the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is 4. Therefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is also 4.\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a number raised to a power\r\ndef units_digit(base, exponent):\r\n    # Extract the units digit of the base\r\n    units_digit_base = base % 10\r\n    # Calculate the units digit of the base raised to the exponent\r\n    units_digit_result = (units_digit_base ** exponent) % 10\r\n    return units_digit_result\r\n\r\n# Calculate the units digit of 18^6\r\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\r\nprint(units_digit_18_6)\n```\n```output\n4\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we can simplify the problem by focusing only on the units digit of the base number, which is 8 in this case. We need to determine the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nLet's observe the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n8^1 & = 8 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^2 & = 64 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n8^3 & = 512 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 2) \\\\\n8^4 & = 4096 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 6) \\\\\n8^5 & = 32768 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 8) \\\\\n8^6 & = 262144 \\quad (\\text{units digit is } 4) \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\) (because \\(6 \\mod 4 = 2\\)), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) is 4.\n\nTherefore, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "To find the units digit of \\(18^6\\), we only need to consider the units digit of the base, which is 8 in this case. We can then determine the units digit of \\(8^6\\).\n\nLet's calculate the units digit step by step:\n\n1. \\(8^1 = 8\\) (units digit is 