duct of these cosines is \\(\\frac{1}{2^7} = \\frac{1}{128}\\).\n\n4. Therefore, the expression simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{128}.\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles\nangles = [sp.Rational(2, 15), sp.Rational(4, 15), sp.Rational(8, 15), sp.Rational(16, 15)]\n\n# Calculate the product of cosines\nproduct = 1\nfor angle in angles:\n    product *= sp.cos(sp.pi * angle)\n\n# Simplify the product\nsimplified_product = sp.simplify(product)\nprint(simplified_product)\n```\n```output\n-cos(pi/15)/2\n```\nIt seems there was a misunderstanding in the initial simplification process. The correct approach involves recognizing that the product of cosines of the form \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{k \\pi}{2n+1} \\right)\\) for \\(k = 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\) is given by \\(\\frac{1}{2^n}\\). Here, we have \\(n = 7\\) (since \\(2n+1 = 15\\)), so the product of these cosines is \\(\\frac{1}{2^7} = \\frac{1}{128}\\).\n\nHowever, the correct simplification should account for the fact that \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{16 \\pi}{15} \\right) = -\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\right)\\), and the product of the cosines should be considered correctly.\n\nLet's correct the approach and simplify the expression again:\n\n1. Recognize that \\(\\cos \\left( \\frac{16 \\pi}{15} \\right) = -\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\right)\\).\n2. Substitute this into the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{16 \\pi}{15} \\right) = \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\left( -\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\right) \\right).\n   \\]\n   This simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   -\\cos \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{2 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{4 \\pi}{15} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{8 \\pi}{15