imeter of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{31}\\).", "To determine the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle, we need to first understand the constraints imposed by the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the following must be true:\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nGiven that two sides of the triangle are each 8 units long, let's denote the sides as \\(a = 8\\), \\(b = 8\\), and \\(c\\) as the third side. The constraints become:\n\n1. \\(8 + 8 > c\\) or \\(16 > c\\)\n2. \\(8 + c > 8\\) or \\(c > 0\\) (which is already implied by \\(c\\) being a whole number)\n3. \\(8 + c > 8\\) or \\(c > 0\\) (which is already implied by \\(c\\) being a whole number)\n\nThe only meaningful constraint here is \\(16 > c\\), meaning \\(c\\) must be a whole number less than 16. The greatest possible value for \\(c\\) is 15. \n\nSo, the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle is \\(8 + 8 + 15 = 31\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code to iterate through all possible values of \\(c\\) and check the perimeter.\n```python\n# Given sides of the triangle\nside1 = 8\nside2 = 8\n\n# Initialize the maximum perimeter\nmax_perimeter = 0\n\n# Iterate through all possible values of the third side\nfor side3 in range(1, 16):\n    # Check if the sides form a valid triangle\n    if side1 + side2 > side3 and side1 + side3 > side2 and side2 + side3 > side1:\n        # Calculate the perimeter\n        perimeter = side1 + side2 + side3\n        # Update the maximum perimeter\n        if perimeter > max_perimeter:\n            max_perimeter = perimeter\n\nprint(max_perimeter)\n```\n```output\n31\n```\nThe greatest possible perimeter of the triangle, given that two sides are each 8 units long and the third side has a whole number length, is \\(\\boxed{31}\\).", "To determine the greatest possible perimeter of a triangle with two sides each being 8 units long and th