on.\n```python\n# Define the function f(x)\ndef f(x):\n    return 2 / (x + 1)\n\n# Define the inverse function f_inv(y)\ndef f_inv(y):\n    return (2 / y) - 1\n\n# Calculate f_inv(1/5)\nresult = f_inv(1/5)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n9.0\n```\nThe value of \\( f^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the reflection matrix that takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix $R$ in 2D can be written in the form:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can use this information to determine $\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $\\sin(2\\theta)$.\n\nFirst, we can write:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can solve for $\\cos(2\\theta)$ and $\\sin(2\\theta)$:\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\n\nNow we can construct the reflection matrix $R$:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection. We do this by multiplying the reflection matrix $R$ by the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\n\nSo the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve the problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's break down the problem into smaller steps:\n\n1. **Determine the reflection matrix:**\n   A reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be represented as:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   where \\(a^2 + b^2 = 1\\).\n\n2. **Use the given vectors to find the reflection matrix:**\n   The vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is transformed to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) by the reflection matrix \\(R\\). So, we have:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   From this, we get two equations:\n   \\[\n   5a = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5b = 3\n   \\]\n   Solving these equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{4}{5} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\frac{3}{5}\n   \\]\n   We can verify that \\(a^2 + b^2 = \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 = 1\\), so the reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under the reflection matrix \\(R\\):**\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python to verify the result:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = sp.Matrix([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = sp.Matrix([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R * v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\nMatrix([[0.200000000000000], [-3.60000000000000]])\n```\nThe output confirms that the reflection of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSo, the reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that maps the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's denote the reflection matrix by $R$. The reflection matrix $R$ should satisfy:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nA reflection matrix in 2D has the general form:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}, \\]\nwhere $a^2 + b^2 = 1$. We can use the given mapping to find the values of $a$ and $b$.\n\nFrom the equation:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\]\nwe get:\n\\[ 5a = 4 \\implies a = \\frac{4}{5}, \\]\n\\[ 5b = 3 \\implies b = \\frac{3}{5}. \\]\n\nSo the reflection matrix $R$ is:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nNow, we can use this matrix to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflected vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection matrix $R$ maps the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that maps the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be written as:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that \\(R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use this information to find \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\).\n\nLet's denote the reflection matrix as \\(R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\\). Then we have:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\