}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the reflection takes \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is equivalent to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's denote the reflection matrix by \\( R \\). The matrix \\( R \\) should satisfy the equation:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nA general reflection matrix in 2D can be written as:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nWe can determine the angle \\( \\theta \\) by solving the equation above. Let's denote the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ as \\( v_1 \\) and the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ as \\( v_2 \\). The reflection matrix \\( R \\) should satisfy:\n\\[ R v_1 = v_2. \\]\n\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nFrom this, we get:\n\\[ 5 \\cos(2\\theta) = 4, \\]\n\\[ 5 \\sin(2\\theta) = 3. \\]\n\nWe can solve these equations to find \\( \\cos(2\\theta) \\) and \\( \\sin(2\\theta) \\):\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5}, \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5}. \\]\n\nNow we can construct the reflection matrix \\( R \\):\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection. Let's denote this vector as \\( v_3 \\). The image of \\( v_3 \\) under the reflection is given by:\n\\[ R v_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nLet's compute this product:\n\\[ R v_3 = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\n\nSo the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector v3\nv3 = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the image of v3 under the reflection\nimage_v3 = R @ v3\nprint(image_v3)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "The reflection that takes $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ can be represented by a matrix. Let's denote this matrix as \\( R \\). The matrix \\( R \\) should satisfy the equation:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nA reflection matrix in 2D has the general form:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nWe can use the given information to determine the values of \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\). By substituting the known vectors into the equation, we get:\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4, \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3. \\]\nFrom these, we can solve for \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\):\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5}, \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5}. \\]\nNow we can construct the reflection matrix \\( R \\):\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nNext, we need to find the reflection of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) using this matrix. We do this by computing:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nLet's compute this step by step.\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{-8 + 9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-6 - 12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{-18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}. \\]\nSo the reflection of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\\).\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}. \\]\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix R\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the reflection matrix that transforms the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ under this reflection.\n\nLet's break down the steps:\n\n1. **Determine the Reflection Matrix:**\n   The reflection matrix $R$ should satisfy $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The general form of a reflection matrix in 2D is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   where $a^2 + b^2 = 1$. We can use the given transformation to find $a$ and $b$.\n\n2. **Set Up the Equations:**\n   From the transformation, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ b & -a \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5a \\\\ 5b \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n   This gives us the equations:\n   \\[\n   5a = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5b = 3\n   \\]\n   Solving these equations, we get:\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{4}{5} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = \\frac{3}{5}\n   \\]\n   We can verify that $a^2 + b^2 = \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{25} + \\frac{9}{25} = 1$, so the matrix is valid.\n\n3. **Construct the Reflection Matrix:**\n   The reflection matrix is:\n   \\[\n   R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Apply the Reflection Matrix to $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:**\n   We need to compute:\n   \\[\n   R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{12}{5} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the reflection takes the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the reflection matrix\nR = np.array([[4/5, 3/5], [3/5, -4/5]])\n\n# Define the vector to be reflected\nv = np.array([-2, 3])\n\n# Compute the reflection of the vector\nreflected_v = R @ v\nprint(reflected_v)\n```\n```output\n[ 0.2 -3.6]\n```\nThe reflection of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}$, which is equivalent to $\\begin{pmatrix} 0.2 \\\\ -3.6 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{18}{5} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to find the reflection matrix that transforms the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\) to \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\). Once we have the reflection matrix, we can use it to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection.\n\nA reflection matrix \\(R\\) in 2D can be written in the form:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between the line of reflection and the x-axis.\n\nGiven that \\(R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we can use this information to find \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\).\n\nLet's solve for \\(\\cos(2\\theta)\\) and \\(\\sin(2\\theta)\\):\n\\[ \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 5\\cos(2\\theta) \\\\ 5\\sin(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nThis gives us the equations:\n\\[ 5\\cos(2\\theta) = 4 \\]\n\\[ 5\\sin(2\\theta) = 3 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ \\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5} \\]\n\\[ \\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5} \\]\nNow we can write the reflection matrix \\(R\\) as:\n\\[ R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\]\nNext, we need to find the image of the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\\) under this reflection:\n\\[ R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5}(-2) + \\frac{3}{5}(3) \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}(-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)(3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{8}{5} + \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5