0.97959184 0.48979592]\n```\nThe projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{49} \\\\ \\frac{48}{49} \\\\ \\frac{24}{49} \\end{pmatrix} \\approx \\begin{pmatrix} 0.32653061 \\\\ 0.97959184 \\\\ 0.48979592 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{49} \\\\ \\frac{48}{49} \\\\ \\frac{24}{49} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]", "To find the projection of vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto vector $\\mathbf{b}$, we use the formula for the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{b}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 8\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 2^2 + 6^2 + 3^2 = 4 + 36 + 9 = 49\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute the values into the projection formula:\n\\[\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}} \\mathbf{a} = \\frac{8}{49} \\mathbf{b} = \\frac{8}{49} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{8 \\cdot 2}{49} \\\\ \\frac{8 \\cdot 6}{49} \\\\ \\frac{8 \\cdot 3}{49} \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{49} \\\\ \\frac{48}{49} \\\\ \\frac{24}{49} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSo, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{16}{49} \\\\ \\frac{48}{49} \\\\ \\frac{24}{49} \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors\nb = np.array([2, 6, 3])\na_dot_b = 8\n\n# Calculate the dot product of b with itself\nb_dot_b = np.dot(b, b)\n\n# Calculate the projection of a onto b\nprojection = (a_dot_b / b_dot_b) * b\nprint(projection)\n```", 'The projection of vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is given by the formula: