lynomial with integer coefficients\npolynomial = sp.Poly(polynomial_equation.lhs - polynomial_equation.rhs, x)\ncoefficients = polynomial.all_coeffs()\n\n# Calculate the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients\nsum_of_abs_coeffs = sum(abs(coeff) for coeff in coefficients)\n\nprint(sum_of_abs_coeffs)\n```\n```output\n0\n```\nIt seems there was an error in the approach. Let's re-evaluate the problem and correct the approach.\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\\). We can use the identity for the sum of arccosines:\n\n\\[\n\\arccos x + \\arccos y = \\arccos(xy - \\sqrt{(1-x^2)(1-y^2)})\n\\]\n\nApplying this identity twice, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x = \\arccos(2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1-x^2)(1-4x^2)})\n\\]\n\nThen:\n\n\\[\n\\arccos(2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1-x^2)(1-4x^2)}) + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1-x^2)(1-4x^2)} + 3x\\sqrt{1 - (2x^2 - \\sqrt{(1-x^2)(1-4x^2)})^2} = -1\n\\]\n\nThis is a complex equation, so let's simplify it step by step. We can use the fact that \\(\\cos(\\arccos x) = x\\), \\(\\cos(\\arccos 2x) = 2x\\), and \\(\\cos(\\arccos 3x) = 3x\\). We can then use the identity \\(\\cos(\\theta_1 + \\theta_2 + \\theta_3) = \\cos \\pi = -1\\) to find the polynomial equation for \\(x\\).\n\nLet's use sympy to find the polynomial equation and the smallest possible value of \\(|a| + |b| + |c| + |d|\\).\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation arccos(x) + arccos(2x) + arccos(3x) = pi\ntheta1 = sp.acos(x)\ntheta2 = sp.acos(2*x)\ntheta3 = sp.acos(3*x)\nequation = sp.Eq(theta1 + theta2 + theta3, sp.pi)\n\n# Use trigonometric identities to simplify the equation\n# cos(theta1 + theta2 + theta3) = cos(pi) = -1\ncos_theta1 = sp.cos(theta1)\ncos_theta2 = sp.cos(theta2)\ncos_theta3 = sp.cos(theta3)\ncos_sum = sp.cos(theta1 + theta2 + theta3)\n\n# Substitute the values of cos(theta1), cos(theta2