ons\nalpha1, alpha2, alpha3, alpha4 = roots\nexpr1 = alpha1*alpha2 + alpha3*alpha4\nexpr2 = alpha1*alpha3 + alpha2*alpha4\nexpr3 = alpha1*alpha4 + alpha2*alpha3\n\n# Create the unordered set\nunordered_set = {expr1, expr2, expr3}\n\n# Print the unordered set\nprint(unordered_set)\n```\n```output\n{-(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)/3 - 2/3 - 2/((-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)*(-1/2 + sqrt(3)*I/2)) - (-1/2 + sqrt(3)*I/2)*(-27 + 3*sqrt(... sqrt(3)*I/2)*(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)), -2/3 - 2/(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3) - (-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)/3 - 2/(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(2/3)}\n```\nThe output is quite complex due to the nature of the roots of the polynomial \\(x^4 + 2x^3 + 2 = 0\\). However, we can simplify our approach by leveraging the properties of polynomials and their roots.\n\nGiven that \\(\\alpha_1, \\alpha_2, \\alpha_3,\\) and \\(\\alpha_4\\) are the roots of the polynomial \\(x^4 + 2x^3 + 2 = 0\\), we can use Vieta's formulas to help us find the values of the expressions \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4\\), \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4\\), and \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\).\n\nFrom Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\\[\n\\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3 + \\alpha_4 = -2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 2\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the values of \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4\\), \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4\\), and \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\). Let's denote these values as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) respectively. Then we have:\n\\[\nA + B + C = \\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can also use the fact that the product of the roots taken two at a time is zero:\n\\[\n(\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4) + (\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4) + (\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3) = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the polynomial is symmetric and the roots are distinct, we can infer that the values of \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\) must be the same up to permutation. Therefore, we can conclude that the unordered set is \\(\\{-2, 1, 1\\}\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python and sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the polynomial equation\npolynomial = x**4 + 2*x**3 + 2\n\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\nroots = sp.solve(polynomial, x)\nroots = [sp.simplify(root) for root in roots]\n\n# Calculate the values of the expressions\nalpha1, alpha2, alpha3, alpha4 = roots\nexpr1 = alpha1*alpha2 + alpha3*alpha4\nexpr2 = alpha1*alpha3 + alpha2*alpha4\nexpr3 = alpha1*alpha4 + alpha2*alpha3\n\n# Create the unordered set\nunordered_set = {expr1, expr2, expr3}\n\n# Simplify the expressions\nsimplified_set = {sp.simplify(expr) for expr in unordered_set}\n\n# Print the simplified unordered set\nprint(simplified_set)\n```\n```output\n{-2/3 - 2/(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3) - (-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)/3 - 2/(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(2/3), -2/3 - 2/3**(2/3)/(1 + sqrt(...)**(1/3) + (-1/2 - sqrt(3)*I/2)*(-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)/3 - 2/((-27 + 3*sqrt(489))**(1/3)*(-1/2 - sqrt(3)*I/2))}, -2/3 + 2/3}\n```\nIt seems that the direct symbolic computation is still resulting in complex expressions. However, we can use the properties of the polynomial and its roots to simplify our approach.\n\nGiven the polynomial \\(x^4 + 2x^3 + 2 = 0\\), we can use Vieta's formulas and the symmetry of the roots to determine the values of \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4\\), \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4\\), and \\(\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\).\n\nFrom Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\\[\n\\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3 + \\al