r less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is $\\boxed{1440}$.', "To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nFirst, let's combine the last two congruences since they have the same remainder:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nwhere 143 is the least common multiple of 11 and 13.\n\nNow we have a system of two congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\n\nWe can express the second congruence as \\( x = 143k + 10 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Substituting this into the first congruence, we get:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), this simplifies to:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 (which is 6, since \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\)), we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nTo find the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010, we need:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nSince \\( m \\) is an integer, the largest possible value for \\( m \\) is 1. Therefore:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nWe can verify that \\( 1726 \\) satisfies all the original congruences:\n\\[ 1726 \\div 7 = 246 \\text{ remainder } 5 \\]\n\\[ 1726 \\div 11 = 156 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\n\\[ 1726 \\div 13 = 132 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\nSo the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is \\( \\boxed{1726} \\).", "To find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions, 