argest integer \\(k\\) can be is 1. Therefore, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\times 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\n\nLet's verify this solution using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions\ndef find_largest_integer():\n    for k in range(1, 2010 // 1001 + 1):\n        x = 1001 * k + 725\n        if x >= 2010:\n            return 1001 * (k - 1) + 725\n\n# Find the largest integer less than 2010\nlargest_integer = find_largest_integer()\nprint(largest_integer)\n```\n```output\n1726\n```\nThe largest integer less than 2010 that has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7, a remainder of 10 when divided by 11, and a remainder of 10 when divided by 13 is \\(\\boxed{1726}\\).", 'To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFirst, we can combine the last two congruences since they both have the same remainder:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\]\nThis is because 11 and 13 are coprime, and the least common multiple (LCM) of 11 and 13 is 143. So, the system of congruences simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nNow, we need to find a solution to this system. We can express the second congruence as:\n\\[\nx = 143k + 10\n\\]\nfor some integer \\(k\\). We substitute this into the first congruence:\n\\[\n143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n143k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\\), we can rewrite this as:\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(-5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\), we get:\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTo solve for \\(k\\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo