nverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, because \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSo \\( k = 5 + 7m \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back, we get:\n\\[\nx = 10 + 143(5 + 7m) = 10 + 715 + 1001m = 725 + 1001m\n\\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010. So we solve:\n\\[\n725 + 1001m < 2010\n\\]\n\\[\n1001m < 1285\n\\]\n\\[\nm < \\frac{1285}{1001}\n\\]\n\\[\nm < 1.283\n\\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) that satisfies this is \\( m = 1 \\). So:\n\\[\nx = 725 + 1001 \\cdot 1 = 1726\n\\]\nTherefore, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is \\( \\boxed{1726} \\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nThis means \\( x = 7k + 5 \\) for some integer \\( k \\), and this value must also satisfy the other two congruences. We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to solve this system. Let's start by finding a common solution to the last two congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nSince both congruences have the same remainder, we can combine them to get:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nwhere 143 is the product of 11 and 13 (since they are coprime).\n\nSo, \\( x = 143m + 10 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Now we need to find \\( m \\) such that:\n\\[ 143m + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSimplifying the left side modulo 7:\n\\[ 143m + 10 \\equiv 6m + 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo we need:\n\\[ 6m + 3 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6m \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 (which is 6, since \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\)):\n\\[ m \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\