\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we can simplify this to:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 (which is 6, since \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\)):\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\times 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 715 + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010, so we solve for the largest \\( m \\) such that:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) that satisfies this is \\( m = 1 \\). So:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\times 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nTherefore, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is \\( \\boxed{1726} \\).', 'To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\), it follows that \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\) because 11 and 13 are coprime. So, we can write:\n\\[ x = 143k + 10 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Now, we need to find \\( k \\) such that \\( 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\). Simplifying, we get:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 143k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nTo find \\( k \\), we need the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6 because \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So, we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nThus, \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting back, we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010, so:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) that satisfies this is \\( m = 1 \\). So, substituting \\( m = 1 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nThus, the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is \\( \\boxed{1726} \\).', "To solve this problem, we need to find the largest integer \\( x \\) less than 2010 that satisfies the following system of congruences:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to solve this system of congruences. First, we note that the moduli 7, 11, and 13 are pairwise coprime, so CRT guarantees a unique solution modulo \\( 7 \\times 11 \\times 13 = 1001 \\).\n\nLet's find a solution to the system of congruences. We can express the solution in the form:\n\\[ x = 1001k + y \\]\nwhere \\( y \\) is a particular solution to the system of congruences and \\( k \\) is an integer. We need to find \\( y \\).\n\nTo find \\( y \\), we can use the method of successive substitutions or a direct approach. We can use the following method to find \\( y \\):\n\n1. Find \\( y \\) such that:\n\\[ y \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nWe can use the method of successive substitutions or the extended Euclidean algorithm to find \\( y \\). Let's use the method of successive substitutions.\n\nFirst, we solve the system:\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nSince 11 and 13 are coprime, the solution to this system is:\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nwhere 143 is the product of 11 and 13.\n\nNow we need to find a solution to:\n\\[ y \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ y \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\n\nWe can write:\n\\[ y = 143m + 10 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Then we need:\n\\[ 143m + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 143m \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[ 6m \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6m \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 (which is 6, since \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\)), we get:\n\\[ m \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ m \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo we can write:\n\\[ m = 7n + 5 \\]\nfor some integer \\( n \\). Substituting back, we get:\n\\[ y = 143(7n + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ y = 1001n + 725 \\]\nSo the general solution to the system of congruences is:\n\\[ x = 1001k + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010. So we need:\n\\[ 1001k + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001k < 1285 \\]\n\\[ k < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nSo the largest possible value for \\( k \\) is 1. Therefore:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\times 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nSo the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions is 1726. Let's verify this using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions\ndef find_largest_integer():\n    for k in range(1, 2010 // 1001 + 1):\n        x = 1001 * k + 725\n        if x < 2010:\n            largest_x = x\n        else:\n            break\n    return largest_x\n\n# Find the largest integer\nlargest_integer = find_largest_integer()\nprint(largest_integer)\n```\n```output\n1726\n```\nThe largest integer less than 2010 that has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7, a remainder of 10 when divided by 11, and a remainder of 10 when divided by 13 is \\(\\boxed{1726}\\).", "Let's denote the number we are looking for by \\( x \\). According to the problem, we have the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nSince \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\), we can combine these two congruences to get:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\]\nwhere \\( 143 = 11 \\times 13 \\). Now, we need to solve the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can express \\( x \\) in terms of the second congruence:\n\\[\nx = 143k + 10\n\\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). Now we substitute this into the first congruence:\n\\[\n143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we have:\n\\[\n6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n6k + 3 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv -3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n6k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6, because \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[\nk \\equiv 4 \\times 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 24 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\nk \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nThus, \\( k = 7m + 3 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = 143(7m + 3) + 10\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1001m + 439\n\\]\nWe need the largest integer less than 2010, so we solve for the maximum \\( m \\):\n\\[\n1001m + 439 < 2010\n\\]\n\\[\n1001m < 1571\n\\]\n\\[\nm < \\frac{1571}{1001}\n\\]\n\\[\nm < 1.569\n\\]\nSince \\( m \\) is an integer, the maximum value for \\( m \\) is 1. So the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the conditions is:\n\\[\nx = 1001 \\times 1 + 439 = 1440\n\\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1990}\n\\]\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to find the largest number less than 2010 that satisfies the given conditions\r\ndef find_largest_number(limit):\r\n    # Start from the largest possible value of m\r\n    for m in range(limit // 1001, -1, -1):\r\n        x = 1001 * m + 439\r\n        if x < limit:\r\n            return x\r\n    return None\r\n\r\n# Define the limit\r\nlimit = 2010\r\n# Find the largest number less than 2010 that satisfies the conditions\r\nlargest_number = find_largest_number(limit)\r\nprint(largest_number)\n```", "Let's solve the problem step by step using Python. We need to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. The remainder when divided by 7 is 5.\n2. The remainder when divided by 11 is 10.\n3. The remainder when divided by 13 is 10.\n\nThese conditions can be expressed as:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\]\n\nFirst, let's find a number that satisfies the last two congruences. Since \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13} \\), it follows that \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\) because 11 and 13 are coprime, and their product is 143. So we can write:\n\\[ x = 143k + 10 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find \\( k \\) such that \\( x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\):\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we can simplify this to:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nTo find \\( k \\), we need the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 is 6 itself because \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo we can write:\n\\[ k = 7m + 5 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010. So we solve:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) can 