{n+1} + b_{n+1} f(1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2(a_n + b_n f(1)) \\]\n\\[ a_{n+1} + b_{n+1} f(1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2a_n - 2b_n f(1) \\]\n\nBy comparing the coefficients of \\( f(1) \\) and the constant terms, we get:\n\\[ b_{n+1} = -2b_n \\]\n\\[ a_{n+1} = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2a_n \\]\n\nWe can solve these recurrence relations to find the general form of \\( a_n \\) and \\( b_n \\). The solution to the first recurrence relation is:\n\\[ b_n = (-2)^{n-1} b_1 \\]\nSince \\( b_1 = 1 \\) (from the initial condition \\( f(1) = f(1) \\)), we have:\n\\[ b_n = (-2)^{n-1} \\]\n\nThe solution to the second recurrence relation can be found by observing the pattern:\n\\[ a_1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ a_2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ a_3 = -4 \\]\n\\[ a_4 = 11 \\]\n\\[ a_5 = -26 \\]\n\nWe can see that:\n\\[ a_{n+1} = (-1)^{n+1} n - 2a_n \\]\nThis recurrence relation can be solved to find a general form, but we don't need the exact form of \\( a_n \\) to find the sum \\( f(1) + f(2) + \\dots + f(1985) \\).\n\nSince \\( f(1986) = f(1) \\), we can use the periodicity of the sequence to find the sum. The sequence \\( f(n) \\) is periodic with period 2, meaning \\( f(n) = f(n + 2) \\). Therefore, we can sum the terms in pairs:\n\n\\[ f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) = (f(1) + f(2)) + (f(3) + f(4)) + \\dots + (f(1983) + f(1984)) + f(1985) \\]\n\nEach pair \\( f(n) + f(n+1) \\) can be calculated using the recurrence relation. Let's calculate the sum of the first pair:\n\n\\[ f(1) + f(2) = f(1) + (1 - 2f(1)) = 1 - f(1) \\]\n\nThe next pair is:\n\n\\[ f(3) + f(4) = (-4 + 4f(1)) + (11 - 8f(1)) = 7 - 4f(1) \\]\n\nWe can see that the sum of each pair is a constant minus a multiple of \\( f(1) \\). Since the sequence is periodic with period 2, the sum of the first 1984 terms is:\n\n\\[ (1 - f(1)) + (7 - 4f(1)) + (19 - 12f(1)) + \\dots + (1983 - 992f(1)) \\]\n\nThe sum of the constants is an arithmetic series:\n\n\\[ 1 + 7 + 19 + \\dots + 1983 \\]\n\nThe sum of the first 992 terms of this series is:\n\n\\[ \\frac{992}{2} (1 