ere \\(a_n\\) and \\(b_n\\) are constants. From the recurrence relation, we can derive the recurrence for \\(a_n\\) and \\(b_n\\):\n\\[f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n)\\]\n\\[a_{n + 1} + b_{n + 1} f(1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2(a_n + b_n f(1))\\]\n\\[a_{n + 1} + b_{n + 1} f(1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2a_n - 2b_n f(1)\\]\nBy comparing the coefficients of \\(f(1)\\) and the constant terms, we get:\n\\[a_{n + 1} = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2a_n\\]\n\\[b_{n + 1} = -2b_n\\]\n\nWe can solve these recurrence relations separately. First, let's solve for \\(b_n\\):\n\\[b_1 = 0\\]\n\\[b_2 = -2b_1 = 0\\]\n\\[b_3 = -2b_2 = 0\\]\n\\[b_4 = -2b_3 = 0\\]\nIt seems that \\(b_n = 0\\) for all \\(n\\). This means that \\(f(n) = a_n\\), and the recurrence relation simplifies to:\n\\[a_{n + 1} = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2a_n\\]\n\nNow, let's solve for \\(a_n\\):\n\\[a_1 = f(1)\\]\n\\[a_2 = (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 - 2a_1 = 1 - 2f(1)\\]\n\\[a_3 = (-1)^3 \\cdot 2 - 2a_2 = -2 - 2(1 - 2f(1)) = -2 - 2 + 4f(1) = -4 + 4f(1)\\]\n\\[a_4 = (-1)^4 \\cdot 3 - 2a_3 = 3 - 2(-4 + 4f(1)) = 3 + 8 - 8f(1) = 11 - 8f(1)\\]\n\\[a_5 = (-1)^5 \\cdot 4 - 2a_4 = -4 - 2(11 - 8f(1)) = -4 - 22 + 16f(1) = -26 + 16f(1)\\]\n\nWe can see that \\(a_n\\) is alternating between a form involving \\(f(1)\\) and a constant term. To find the sum \\(f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985)\\), we need to sum the sequence \\(a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\dots + a_{1985}\\).\n\nGiven that \\(f(1) = f(1986)\\), we can use the fact that the sequence is periodic with period 2. This means that \\(a_{1986} = a_1 = f(1)\\), and the sum of the sequence can be simplified.\n\nLet's compute the sum of the first 1985 terms:\n\\[S = a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \\dots + a_{1985}\\]\nSince the sequence is periodic with period 2, we can group the terms in pairs:\n\\[S = (a_1 + a_2) + (a_3 + a_4) + \\dots + (a_{1983} + a_{1984}) + a_{1985}\\]\nEach pair \\((a_{2k-1} + a_{2k})\\) can be computed as:\n\\[a_{2k-1} + a_{2k} = (-1)^{2k} (2k-1) - 2a_{2k-1} + (-1)^{2k+1} (2k) - 2a_{2k}\\]\n\\[= (2k-1) - 2a_