he total number of students is 35, so we have:\n\\[ G + B = 35 \\]\nWe also know that \\( G \\) is a multiple of 13 and \\( G > B \\). Since \\( G \\) is a multiple of 13, the possible values for \\( G \\) are 13, 26, and 39 (but 39 is not possible because the total number of students is only 35). So, the possible values for \\( G \\) are 13 and 26.\n\nLet's check each case:\n\n1. If \\( G = 13 \\), then \\( B = 35 - 13 = 22 \\). But \\( G \\) is not greater than \\( B \\) in this case, so this is not a valid solution.\n2. If \\( G = 26 \\), then \\( B = 35 - 26 = 9 \\). In this case, \\( G > B \\), so this is a valid solution.\n\nThus, the number of boys at the meeting is 9. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Total number of students\ntotal_students = 35\n\n# Possible values for the number of girls (multiples of 13)\npossible_girls = [13, 26]\n\n# Check each possible value for girls\nfor girls in possible_girls:\n    boys = total_students - girls\n    if girls > boys:\n        number_of_boys = boys\n        break\n\nprint(number_of_boys)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of boys at the meeting is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to find the number of boys at the meeting. Let's break down the problem step by step:\n\n1. Let \\( G \\) be the number of girls and \\( B \\) be the number of boys.\n2. We know that the total number of students is 35, so \\( G + B = 35 \\).\n3. We also know that \\( G \\) is a multiple of 13 and \\( G > B \\).\n\nFirst, let's list the multiples of 13 that are less than 35: 13 and 26. Since \\( G > B \\), \\( G \\) cannot be 13 (because \\( B \\) would be 22, and 13 is not greater than 22). So, \\( G \\) must be 26. If \\( G = 26 \\), then \\( B = 35 - 26 = 9 \\).\n\nLet's check if this satisfies all the conditions:\n- \\( G = 26 \\) is a multiple of 13.\n- \\( G > B \\) (26 > 9).\n\nSince all conditions are satisfied, the number of boys at the meeting is \\( B = 9 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python 