s verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\n# Compute the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83\na = 17\nb = 83\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, b)\n\n# Since a and b are coprime, gcd should be 1\nif gcd == 1:\n    # x might be negative, so we take it modulo b to get a positive value\n    mod_inverse = x % b\nelse:\n    mod_inverse = None  # In case gcd is not 1, though it shouldn't be for coprime numbers\n\nprint(mod_inverse)\n```\n```output\n44\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(17 \\pmod{83}\\) is \\(\\boxed{44}\\).", "To find the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that \\( 17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83} \\). This is equivalent to finding \\( x \\) such that \\( 17x - 83k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a typical application of the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nThe steps are as follows:\n\n1. Use the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 17 and 83.\n2. Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n3. If the gcd is 1, the coefficient of 17 in that linear combination will be the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83.\n\nLet's start by implementing the Extended Euclidean Algorithm in Python.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\n# Compute the gcd and the coefficients\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(17, 83)\n\n# Since we are looking for the modular inverse, it should exist only if the gcd is 1\nif gcd == 1:\n    # Ensure the result is in the range from 0 to 82\n    modular_inverse = x % 83\nelse:\n    modular_inverse = None\n\nprint(modular_inverse)\n```\n```output\n48\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(17\\) modulo \\(83\\) is 