ln{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{7}}{\\ln{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{7}} = \\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{2}}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln{8} = \\ln{2^3} = 3\\ln{2}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln{8}}{\\ln{2}} = \\frac{3\\ln{2}}{\\ln{2}} = 3\n\\]\n\nSo the value of the expression is \\(3\\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the value of the expression using the change of base formula\nlog_2_3 = math.log(3, 2)\nlog_3_4 = math.log(4, 3)\nlog_4_5 = math.log(5, 4)\nlog_5_6 = math.log(6, 5)\nlog_6_7 = math.log(7, 6)\nlog_7_8 = math.log(8, 7)\n\nresult = log_2_3 * log_3_4 * log_4_5 * log_5_6 * log_6_7 * log_7_8\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n2.9999999999999996\n```\nThe calculated result is very close to 3, confirming our analytical solution. The small discrepancy is due to floating-point precision limitations in numerical computations.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]", "We can use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that $\\log_a{b} = \\frac{\\log_c{b}}{\\log_c{a}}$ for any positive base $c$. By applying this formula, we can rewrite each logarithm in the product with a common base, such as base 2.\n\nLet's rewrite each term in the product using the change of base formula with base 2:\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8} = \\frac{\\log_2{3}}{\\log_2{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{4}}{\\log_2{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{5}}{\\log_2{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{6}}{\\log_2{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{7}}{\\log_2{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{8}}{\\log_2{7}}.\n\\]\n\nWe can see that this is a telescoping product, where most terms will cancel out:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_2{3}}{1} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{4}}{\\log_2{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{5}}{\\log_2{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{6}}{\\log_2{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{7}}{\\log_2{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{8}}{\\log_2{7}} = \\log_2{8}.\n\\]\n\nSince $\\log_2{8} = \\log_2{2^3} = 3$, the value of the expression is 3. L