fy the expression. The change of base formula states that $\\log_a{b} = \\frac{\\log_c{b}}{\\log_c{a}}$ for any positive base $c$. If we use base 2 for all the logarithms, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8} = \\frac{\\log_2{3}}{\\log_2{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{4}}{\\log_2{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{5}}{\\log_2{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{6}}{\\log_2{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{7}}{\\log_2{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{8}}{\\log_2{7}}\n\\]\n\nNotice that this is a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{\\log_2{3}}{1} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{4}}{\\log_2{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{5}}{\\log_2{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{6}}{\\log_2{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{7}}{\\log_2{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log_2{8}}{\\log_2{7}} = \\log_2{8}\n\\]\n\nSince $8 = 2^3$, we have $\\log_2{8} = 3$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Calculate the product of the logarithms\nresult = (math.log(3, 2) * math.log(4, 3) * math.log(5, 4) * math.log(6, 5) * math.log(7, 6) * math.log(8, 7))\n\nprint(result)\n```", "We can use the change of base formula for logarithms to simplify the expression. The change of base formula states that $\\log_a{b} = \\frac{\\log_c{b}}{\\log_c{a}}$ for any positive base $c$. If we choose a common base, say base 10, then we can rewrite each logarithm in the expression as follows:\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{3}}{\\log_{10}{2}}, \\quad \\log_3{4} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{4}}{\\log_{10}{3}}, \\quad \\log_4{5} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{5}}{\\log_{10}{4}}, \\quad \\log_5{6} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{6}}{\\log_{10}{5}}, \\quad \\log_6{7} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{7}}{\\log_{10}{6}}, \\quad \\log_7{8} = \\frac{\\log_{10}{8}}{\\log_{10}{7}}.\n\\]\nWhen we multiply these together, we get:\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8} = \\left(\\frac{\\lo