ability of obtaining the face opposite face \\(F\\) is less than \\(1/6\\).\n   - The probability of obtaining each of the other faces is \\(1/6\\).\n   - The sum of the numbers on each pair of opposite faces is 7.\n   - When two such dice are rolled, the probability of obtaining a sum of 7 is \\(\\frac{47}{288}\\).\n\n2. **Determine the probabilities:**\n   - Let the probability of face \\(F\\) be \\(p\\).\n   - The probability of the face opposite \\(F\\) (let's call it \\(F'\\)) be \\(q\\).\n   - The probability of each of the other faces (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 except \\(F\\) and \\(F'\\)) be \\(1/6\\).\n\n3. **Set up the equations:**\n   - The sum of the probabilities of all faces must be 1:\n     \\[\n     p + q + 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = 1\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     p + q + \\frac{2}{3} = 1 \\implies p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n   - The probability of obtaining a sum of 7 when two dice are rolled is given by:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{sum of 7}) = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     The pairs that sum to 7 are (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1), and (F, F'). So, the probability is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{sum of 7}) = p \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot p + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} + q \\cdot \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, we get:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{2p}{6} + \\frac{4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{36}}{1} + \\frac{q}{6} = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\frac{p}{3} + \\frac{q}{6} + \\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{47}{288}\n     \\]\n     Multiplying through by 288 to clear the denominators, we get:\n     \\[\n     96p + 48q + 32 = 47\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     96p + 48q = 15\n     \\]\n     Dividing through by 3, we get:\n     \\[\n     32p + 16q = 5\n     \\]\n     Since \\(p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\\), we can solve these equations simultaneously. Let's solve them using Python.\n\n4. **Solve the equations:**\n   - We have \\(p + q = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and \\(32p + 16q = 5\\).\n   - We can solve these equations using Python.\n\nLet's implement this in Python to find the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\).\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variables\np, q = symbols('p q')\n\n# Define the equations\neq1 = Eq(p + q, 1/3)\neq2 = Eq(32*p + 16*q, 5)\n\n# Solve the equations\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (p, q))\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{p: 0.125000000000000, q: 0.166666666666667}\n```\nThe solution to the equations is \\( p = \\frac{1}{8} \\) and \\( q = \\frac{1}{6} \\). However, this solution does not satisfy the condition that \\( p > \\frac{1}{6} \\) and \\( q < \\frac{1}{6} \\). Let's recheck the equations and solve them again to ensure we get the correct values.\n\nWe have:\n\\[ p + q = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ 32p + 16q = 5 \\]\n\nLet's solve these equations again, ensuring that \\( p > \\frac{1}{6} \\) and \\( q < \\frac{1}{6} \\). We'll use the same equations but check the conditions more carefully.\n\nLet's solve the equations again using Python.\n```python\n# Define the equations again\r\neq1 = Eq(p + q, 1/3)\r\neq2 = Eq(32*p + 16*q, 5)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (p, q))\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'Eq' is not defined\n```\nIt seems there was an issue with the previous code execution. Let's try solving the equations again using Python to ensure we get the correct values for \\( p \\) and \\( q \\).\n\nWe'll solve the equations:\n\\[ p + q = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\\[ 32p + 16q = 5 \\]\n\nLet's solve these equations again using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\np, q = symbols('p q')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(p + q, 1/3)\r\neq2 = Eq(32*p + 16*q, 5)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (p, q))\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{p: 0.125000000000000, q: 0.166666666666667}\n```\n\nReach