)(y-4) + 4(y-1)(y-2)(y-3).\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\\[\n(y-2)(y-3)(y-4) = y^3 - 9y^2 + 26y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n2(y-1)(y-3)(y-4) = 2(y^3 - 8y^2 + 19y - 12) = 2y^3 - 16y^2 + 38y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n3(y-1)(y-2)(y-4) = 3(y^3 - 7y^2 + 14y - 8) = 3y^3 - 21y^2 + 42y - 24,\n\\]\n\\[\n4(y-1)(y-2)(y-3) = 4(y^3 - 6y^2 + 11y - 6) = 4y^3 - 24y^2 + 44y - 24.\n\\]\n\nAdding these together, we get:\n\\[\ny^3 - 9y^2 + 26y - 24 + 2y^3 - 16y^2 + 38y - 24 + 3y^3 - 21y^2 + 42y - 24 + 4y^3 - 24y^2 + 44y - 24 = 10y^3 - 70y^2 + 150y - 120.\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{10y^3 - 70y^2 + 150y - 120}{(y-1)(y-2)(y-3)(y-4)} = 2010x - 4.\n\\]\n\nSince the left-hand side is a rational function, we need to find the roots of the polynomial equation. The roots of the numerator \\(10y^3 - 70y^2 + 150y - 120 = 0\\) will give us the values of \\(y\\). We can solve this polynomial equation using Python.\n\nLet's solve it using Python:\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\ny = sp.symbols('y')\n\n# Define the polynomial equation\npolynomial = 10*y**3 - 70*y**2 + 150*y - 120\n\n# Solve the polynomial equation\nsolutions_y = sp.solve(polynomial, y)\nprint(solutions_y)\n```\n```output\n[2 + (-1/2 - sqrt(3)*I/2)*(sqrt(687)/5 + 6)**(1/3) + 1/((-1/2 - sqrt(3)*I/2)*(sqrt(687)/5 + 6)**(1/3)), 2 + 1/((-1/2 + sqrt(3)*I...6)**(1/3)) + (-1/2 + sqrt(3)*I/2)*(sqrt(687)/5 + 6)**(1/3), (sqrt(687)/5 + 6)**(-1/3) + (sqrt(687)/5 + 6)**(1/3) + 2]\n```\nThe solutions to the polynomial equation \\(10y^3 - 70y^2 + 150y - 120 = 0\\) are:\n\\[\ny = 2 + \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}i}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{687}}{5} + 6\\right)^{1/3} + \\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}i}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{687}}{5} + 6\\right)^{1/3}},\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2 + \\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}i}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{687}}{5} + 6\\right)^{1/3}} + \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}i}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{687}}{5} + 6\\right)^{1/