ots by $r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n$. Then, we have:\n\\[ r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}. \\]\nAlso, the product of the roots is given by $(-1)^n \\frac{a_0}{a_n} = (-1)^n \\frac{66}{2} = (-1)^n \\cdot 33$. Since the roots are integers, the product of the roots must be $\\pm 33$. The possible factorizations of $\\pm 33$ into distinct integers are:\n\\[ \\pm 33 = \\pm 1 \\cdot (\\pm 33), \\]\n\\[ \\pm 33 = \\pm 1 \\cdot \\pm 3 \\cdot \\pm 11. \\]\nWe need to find the combination of roots that minimizes $|a_{n-1}|$. Let's consider the different cases:\n\n1. If the roots are $1$ and $-33$, then the sum of the roots is $1 - 33 = -32$, so $a_{n-1} = 64$.\n2. If the roots are $-1$ and $33$, then the sum of the roots is $-1 + 33 = 32$, so $a_{n-1} = -64$.\n3. If the roots are $1$, $-1$, $3$, and $-11$, then the sum of the roots is $1 - 1 + 3 - 11 = -8$, so $a_{n-1} = 16$.\n4. If the roots are $-1$, $1$, $-3$, and $11$, then the sum of the roots is $-1 + 1 - 3 + 11 = 8$, so $a_{n-1} = -16$.\n\nThe smallest absolute value of $a_{n-1}$ from the above cases is $16$. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Function to calculate the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\ndef min_abs_a_n_minus_1(a_0, a_n):\n    # Possible factorizations of a_0 / a_n\n    factorizations = []\n    if a_0 > 0:\n        factorizations.append((1, 33))\n        factorizations.append((-1, -33))\n        factorizations.append((1, 3, 11))\n        factorizations.append((-1, -1, -3, -11))\n    else:\n        factorizations.append((-1, 33))\n        factorizations.append((1, -33))\n        factorizations.append((-1, -3, -11))\n        factorizations.append((1, 1, 3, 11))\n    \n    min_abs_value = float('inf')\n    \n    for roots in factorizations:\n        # Calculate the sum of the roots\n        sum_of_roots = sum(roots)\n        # Calculate a_{n-1}\n        a_n_minus_1 = -a_n * sum_of_roots\n        # Update the minimum absolute value\n        min_abs_value = min(mi