ent \\(a_n = 2\\), the product of the roots (with their signs taken into account) must be \\(\\pm 66 / 2 = \\pm 33\\). We need to find the distinct integer roots whose product is \\(\\pm 33\\). The possible factorizations of 33 are:\n\\[33 = 1 \\cdot 33, \\quad 33 = -1 \\cdot -33, \\quad 33 = 3 \\cdot 11, \\quad 33 = -3 \\cdot -11.\\]\n\nThe polynomial can be written as \\(f(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n)\\), where \\(r_i\\) are the roots. The coefficient \\(a_{n-1}\\) is given by the sum of the roots multiplied by the leading coefficient 2. So, we need to find the combination of roots that minimizes \\(|a_{n-1}|\\).\n\nLet's consider the possible combinations of roots and calculate \\(a_{n-1}\\):\n\n1. If the roots are \\(1, 33\\), then \\(a_{n-1} = 2(1 + 33) = 68\\).\n2. If the roots are \\(-1, -33\\), then \\(a_{n-1} = 2(-1 - 33) = -68\\).\n3. If the roots are \\(3, 11\\), then \\(a_{n-1} = 2(3 + 11) = 28\\).\n4. If the roots are \\(-3, -11\\), then \\(a_{n-1} = 2(-3 - 11) = -28\\).\n\nThe least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is 28. Let's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the possible roots and calculate the corresponding a_{n-1} values\nroots_combinations = [(1, 33), (-1, -33), (3, 11), (-3, -11)]\na_n_minus_1_values = [2 * (r1 + r2) for r1, r2 in roots_combinations]\n\n# Calculate the absolute values of a_{n-1} and find the minimum\nabs_a_n_minus_1_values = [abs(val) for val in a_n_minus_1_values]\nmin_abs_a_n_minus_1 = min(abs_a_n_minus_1_values)\nprint(min_abs_a_n_minus_1)\n```\n```output\n28\n```\nThe least possible value of \\(|a_{n-1}|\\) is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).", "To find the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) for the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with distinct integer roots, we need to use Vieta's formulas. According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of the polynomial (with the leading coefficient factored out) is equal to \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}\\). In this case, the sum of the roots is \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}\\).\n\nGiven that the polynomial has integer roots and the constant term \\( a_0 = 66 \\), the roots must be factors of 66. The factors of 66 are:\n\\[ \\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 3, \\pm 6, \\pm 11, \\pm 22, \\pm 33, \\pm 66. \\]\n\nTo minimize \\( |a_{n-1}| \\), we need to find the combination of distinct factors of 66 that sum to the smallest possible value. Since \\( a_n = 2 \\), the sum of the roots must be \\(-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}\\), so \\( a_{n-1} = -2 \\times \\text{(sum of the roots)} \\).\n\nLet's find the combination of distinct factors of 66 that sum to the smallest possible value. We can use Python to check all possible combinations of these factors.\n\nFirst, we will generate all possible combinations of the factors of 66 and calculate the sum of each combination. Then, we will find the combination that gives the smallest absolute value for \\( a_{n-1} \\).\n```python\nimport itertools\nimport math\n\n# Factors of 66\nfactors = [1, -1, 2, -2, 3, -3, 6, -6, 11, -11, 22, -22, 33, -33, 66, -66]\n\n# Function to find the minimum absolute value of a_{n-1}\ndef find_min_abs_an1(factors):\n    min_abs_an1 = float('inf')\n    best_combination = None\n    n = len(factors)\n    \n    # Check all combinations of factors\n    for r in range(1, n + 1):\n        for combination in itertools.combinations(factors, r):\n            if math.prod(combination) == 66:  # Check if the product of the combination is 66\n                sum_of_roots = sum(combination)\n                abs_an1 = abs(2 * sum_of_roots)\n                if abs_an1 < min_abs_an1:\n                    min_abs_an1 = abs_an1\n                    best_combination = combination\n    \n    return min_abs_an1, best_combination\n\nmin_abs_an1, best_combination = find_min_abs_an1(factors)\nprint((min_abs_an1, best_combination))\n```\n```output\n(22, (-1, 2, 3, 11))\n```\nThe combination of roots \\((-1, 2, 3, 11)\\) gives the smallest possible v