t \\mathbf{u} = 2v_1 + v_2 = 0\n\\]\nThis equation represents a line in the \\(v_1\\)-\\(v_2\\) plane. We can rearrange it to the form \\(y = mx + b\\):\n\\[\nv_2 = -2v_1\n\\]\nSo the equation of the line is:\n\\[\ny = -2x\n\\]\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nv1, v2 = sp.symbols('v1 v2')\n\n# Define the vector u\nu = sp.Matrix([2, 1])\n\n# Define the vector v\nv = sp.Matrix([v1, v2])\n\n# Calculate the dot product of v and u\ndot_product = v.dot(u)\n\n# Solve the equation dot_product = 0\nsolution = sp.solve(dot_product, v2)\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n[-2*v1]\n```\nThe solution confirms that the equation of the line is \\(v_2 = -2v_1\\). Therefore, the equation of the line in the form \\(y = mx + b\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{y = -2x}\n\\]", "The projection of a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ is given by\n\\[\n\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{v} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u}.\n\\]\nWe want this projection to be the zero vector, i.e.,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}}{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}} \\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis implies that $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 0$. So we need to find the dot product of $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$, which is\n\\[\n2v_1 + v_2 = 0.\n\\]\nThis equation represents a line in the $v_1$-$v_2$ plane. To express this line in the form $y = mx + b$, we solve for $v_2$ in terms of $v_1$:\n\\[\nv_2 = -2v_1.\n\\]\nSo the equation of the line is $y = -2x$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nv1, v2 = sp.symbols('v1 v2')\r\n\r\n# Define the equation for the dot product to be zero\r\nequation = 2